{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760694952,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e012d95e002oe03iUb68XwdDq","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e012a9e1001eZsPxCfv3XFVDL","type":"tool","id":"call_caa677f0168a4fb688f9a8de","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md, lines 1-363\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\"\n3: description: \"Canonical sk-doc manual testing playbook for the Skill Advisor manual scenario corpus across the native command surface, runtime hooks, compatibility, daemon state, indexing, lifecycle, scorer and Python compatibility workflows.\"\n4: version: 0.8.12.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\n8: \n9: This document combines the canonical manual-validation contract for the `skill_advisor` package into a single reference. The root playbook acts as the operator directory, review protocol and orchestration guide while the per-feature files carry scenario-specific execution truth.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: This playbook package follows the sk-doc manual testing playbook template. The current corpus is the historical operator scenario set merged into the canonical manual-testing package on 2026-05-07, with former `SAD-NNN` scenarios absorbed into their current `NC` and `CL` homes.\n14: \n15: Canonical package artifacts:\n16: - `manual-testing-playbook.md`\n17: - `native-cli-tools/`\n18: - `cli-hooks-and-plugin/`\n19: - `compat-and-disable/`\n20: - `operator-h5/`\n21: - `auto-update-daemon/`\n22: - `auto-indexing/`\n23: - `lifecycle-routing/`\n24: - `scorer-fusion/`\n25: - `python-compat/`\n26: \n27: ### Result persistence\n28: \n29: <!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n30: A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome, its reason and its evidence paths are recorded by hand in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. The first recorded run creates that folder, because the wrapper that once wrote these records was retired and no renderer writes them now.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n35: \n36: This playbook provides 53 scenario files across 9 categories, one of them a live conversation scenario, validating the Skill Advisor surface. Scenario IDs use a multi-prefix scheme: `NC` for the native command surface, `CL` for CLI hooks plus plugin behavior, `CP` for compatibility plus disable controls, `OP` for operator H5 states, `AU` for auto-update daemon behavior, `AI` for auto-indexing, `LC` for lifecycle routing, `SC` for scorer fusion and `PC` for Python compatibility.\n37: \n38: > **Numbering note (gap-09).** The directory layout skips slot `09--*` between `scorer-fusion` and `python-compat`. This mirrors the `feature-catalog/` 05-gap pattern and is an intentional historical reservation from initial scaffold design. The gap is preserved to keep spec-folder cross-reference stability across packets. Do not renumber.\n39: \n40: Coverage note (2026-05-07): the prior operator scenario corpus now lives under `manual-testing-playbook/` as the canonical manual validation package. Former `SAD-NNN` IDs are deprecated plus mapped to current feature files in section 18. The source root index describes 42 scenarios. The category tree holds 53 scenario files named by feature, none of them `NNN-*.md`, and still no `CL-002`. This root playbook reflects the live file inventory.\n41: \n42: ### Realistic Test Model\n43: \n44: 1. A realistic user request is given to an operator or runtime.\n45: 2. The operator decides whether to call the native command through the CLI, run a prompt-time hook, execute compatibility shims or inspect daemon/indexing behavior.\n46: 3. The operator captures command transcripts, CLI JSON envelopes, hook stdout/stderr and final verdicts.\n47: 4. The scenario passes only when observed output is prompt-safe, user-visible and consistent with the documented feature contract.\n48: \n49: ### What Each Feature File Should Explain\n50: \n51: - The realistic user request or operational state that should trigger the behavior.\n52: - The deterministic setup and execution steps.\n53: - The expected signals and failure modes.\n54: - The desired user-visible outcome.\n55: - The implementation, automated test or catalog anchors that justify the scenario.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. GLOBAL PRECONDITIONS\n60: \n61: 1. Working directory is the repository root.\n62: 2. Node.js and Python 3 are available.\n63: 3. The advisor runtime build is current:\n64: \n65: ```bash\n66: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 4. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset unless a scenario explicitly sets it.\n70: 5. Operators can run the documented advisor CLI and Node/Python commands from the repo root.\n71: 6. Destructive or stateful scenarios use a disposable workspace copy unless the scenario explicitly validates the live checkout.\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 3. GLOBAL EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n76: \n77: - Scenario ID and per-feature file path.\n78: - User request or operational condition used.\n79: - Exact command transcript or CLI call payload.\n80: - Full JSON output or a focused excerpt containing asserted fields.\n81: - Exit code for shell commands.\n82: - Hook stdout and stderr captured separately when hook scripts are involved.\n83: - Final user-facing verdict with rationale.\n84: - Triage notes for every `FAIL`, `PARTIAL` or `SKIP`.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. DETERMINISTIC COMMAND NOTATION\n89: \n90: - CLI commands shown as `bash: <command>`.\n91: - CLI calls shown as `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command> --format json`.\n92: - Hook stdin payloads shown as JSON piped into the compiled hook script.\n93: - `->` separates sequential steps inside one scenario contract.\n94: - File references use repo-root-relative paths unless otherwise stated.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. REVIEW PROTOCOL AND RELEASE READINESS\n99: \n100: ### Inputs Required\n101: \n102: 1. `manual-testing-playbook.md`.\n103: 2. Referenced per-feature files under `manual-testing-playbook/NN__category_name/`.\n104: 3. Scenario execution evidence.\n105: 4. Feature-to-scenario coverage map.\n106: 5. Triage notes for all non-pass outcomes.\n107: \n108: ### Scenario Acceptance Rules\n109: \n110: For each executed scenario, check:\n111: \n112: 1. Preconditions were satisfied.\n113: 2. Prompt and command sequence were executed as written.\n114: 3. Expected signals are present in the captured output.\n115: 4. Evidence is complete and readable.\n116: 5. Raw prompt text is not leaked into diagnostics, attribution, cache metadata or trust-state fields unless the scenario explicitly validates input echo behavior.\n117: 6. Outcome rationale is explicit and references the user-visible behavior.\n118: \n119: Scenario verdict:\n120: - `PASS`: all acceptance checks true.\n121: - `PARTIAL`: core behavior works but non-critical evidence or metadata is incomplete.\n122: - `FAIL`: expected behavior missing, contradictory output appears, prompt text leaks or a critical check failed.\n123: - `SKIP`: a real sandbox, dependency or runtime blocker prevents execution and is documented.\n124: \n125: ### Feature Verdict Rules\n126: \n127: - `PASS`: all mapped scenarios for the feature are `PASS`.\n128: - `PARTIAL`: at least one mapped scenario is `PARTIAL`, none are `FAIL`.\n129: - `FAIL`: any mapped scenario is `FAIL`.\n130: \n131: ### Release Readiness Rule\n132: \n133: Release is `READY` only when all 53 scenario files are `PASS` or have an approved `SKIP` with a real blocker and no prompt-safety, rebuild, daemon, indexing, lifecycle, scorer or compatibility failure remains unresolved.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. SUB-AGENT ORCHESTRATION AND WAVE PLANNING\n138: \n139: ### Purpose\n140: \n141: This section records wave planning for the canonical Skill Advisor manual test package. The scenario corpus is large enough to split across operators while keeping category ownership clear.\n142: \n143: ### Operational Rules\n144: \n145: 1. Probe build state first with the advisor runtime build command.\n146: 2. Run native command-surface scenarios before hook scenarios so runtime failures can be separated from scorer or command failures.\n147: 3. Run rebuild and daemon scenarios in disposable workspaces when validating repair or generated state.\n148: 4. Assign explicit scenario IDs before parallel execution.\n149: 5. Keep stdout, stderr and CLI JSON evidence in scenario-specific files under `/tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/`.\n150: 6. After each wave, record verdicts and blockers before starting the next wave.\n151: \n152: ### Recommended Wave Plan\n153: \n154: - **Wave 1**: `NC-001..NC-012` native command-surface behavior.\n155: - **Wave 2**: `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-010` runtime hooks, the OpenCode plugin, CLI fallback and the goal plugin.\n156: - **Wave 3**: `CP-001..CP-004` compatibility and disable controls.\n157: - **Wave 4**: `OP-001..OP-003` H5 operator states.\n158: - **Wave 5**: `AU-001..AU-005` auto-update daemon behavior.\n159: - **Wave 6**: `AI-001..AI-007` auto-indexing behavior.\n160: - **Wave 7**: `LC-001..LC-005` lifecycle routing.\n161: - **Wave 8**: `SC-001..SC-005` scorer fusion.\n162: - **Wave 9**: `PC-001..PC-005` Python compatibility.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. NATIVE COMMAND SURFACE\n167: \n168: This category validates native command-surface scenarios `NC-001..NC-012`.\n169: \n170: | ID | Scenario | File |\n171: |---|---|---|\n172: | NC-001 | Native advisor_recommend Happy Path | [native-recommend-happy-path.md](native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md) |\n173: | NC-002 | Native advisor_status Transitions | [native-status-transitions.md](native-cli-tools/native-status-transitions.md) |\n174: | NC-003 | Native advisor_validate Slice Bundle | [native-validate-slices.md](native-cli-tools/native-validate-slices.md) |\n175: | NC-004 | Ambiguous Brief Rendering | [ambiguous-brief-rendering.md](native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md) |\n176: | NC-005 | Lifecycle Redirect Metadata | [lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md](native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md) |\n177: | NC-006 | Advisor Status and Rebuild Separation | [advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md](native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md) |\n178: | NC-007 | Skill Graph Status | [skill-graph-status.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-status.md) |\n179: | NC-008 | Skill Graph Query | [skill-graph-query.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-query.md) |\n180: | NC-009 | Skill Graph Validate | [skill-graph-validate.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-validate.md) |\n181: | NC-010 | Shadow-Delta Sink Opt-In | [shadow-delta-sink.md](native-cli-tools/shadow-delta-sink.md) |\n182: | NC-011 | Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command | [hub-command-attachment.md](native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md) |\n183: | NC-012 | A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered | [command-offer-fit-gates.md](native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md) |\n184: \n185: ---\n186: \n187: ## 8. CLI HOOKS AND PLUGIN\n188: \n189: This category validates cli hooks and plugin scenarios `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-010`.\n190: \n191: | ID | Scenario | File |\n192: |---|---|---|\n193: | CL-001 | Claude Code UserPromptSubmit Hook | [001-claude-user-prompt-submit.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md) |\n194: | CL-004 | OpenCode Native Hooks And Wrapper Fallback | 004-opencode-hook-and-wrapper.md (not yet authored) |\n195: | CL-005 | OpenCode Plugin | [005-opencode-plugin-bridge.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/opencode-plugin-bridge.md) |\n196: | CL-006 | skill-advisor CLI Fallback Surface (028) | [006-skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md) |\n197: | CL-007 | Goal OpenCode Plugin | [goal-opencode-plugin.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) |\n198: | CL-008 | Goal OpenCode Later-Turn Injection | [goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md) |\n199: | CL-009 | The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session | [brief-command-policy-word.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md) |\n200: | CL-010 | The Vibe Brief Names Each Command's Document | [vibe-command-documents.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/vibe-command-documents.md) |\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## 9. COMPAT AND DISABLE\n205: \n206: This category validates compat and disable scenarios `CP-001..CP-004`.\n207: \n208: | ID | Scenario | File |\n209: |---|---|---|\n210: | CP-001 | Python Shim --stdin Mode | [001-python-shim-stdin.md](compat-and-disable/python-shim-stdin.md) |\n211: | CP-002 | Force Local And Force Native Toggles | [002-force-local-force-native.md](compat-and-disable/force-local-force-native.md) |\n212: | CP-003 | Global Disable Flag | [003-global-disable-flag.md](compat-and-disable/global-disable-flag.md) |\n213: | CP-004 | Daemon Absent Fallback | [004-daemon-absent-fallback.md](compat-and-disable/daemon-absent-fallback.md) |\n214: \n215: ---\n216: \n217: ## 10. OPERATOR H5\n218: \n219: This category validates operator h5 scenarios `OP-001..OP-003`.\n220: \n221: | ID | Scenario | File |\n222: |---|---|---|\n223: | OP-001 | Degraded Daemon | [001-degraded-daemon.md](operator-h5/degraded-daemon.md) |\n224: | OP-002 | Quarantined Daemon | [002-quarantined-daemon.md](operator-h5/quarantined-daemon.md) |\n225: | OP-003 | Unavailable Daemon | [003-unavailable-daemon.md](operator-h5/unavailable-daemon.md) |\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 11. AUTO UPDATE DAEMON\n230: \n231: This category validates auto update daemon scenarios `AU-001..AU-005`.\n232: \n233: | ID | Scenario | File |\n234: |---|---|---|\n235: | AU-001 | Chokidar Watcher Narrow Scope | [001-watcher-narrow-scope.md](auto-update-daemon/watcher-narrow-scope.md) |\n236: | AU-002 | Workspace Single-Writer Lease | [002-lease-single-writer.md](auto-update-daemon/lease-single-writer.md) |\n237: | AU-003 | Daemon Lifecycle and SIGTERM | [003-daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md](auto-update-daemon/daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md) |\n238: | AU-004 | Generation-Tagged Snapshot Publication | [004-generation-publication.md](auto-update-daemon/generation-publication.md) |\n239: | AU-005 | Rebuild From Source on Corrupt SQLite | [005-rebuild-from-source.md](auto-update-daemon/rebuild-from-source.md) |\n240: \n241: ---\n242: \n243: ## 12. AUTO INDEXING\n244: \n245: This category validates auto indexing scenarios `AI-001..AI-007`.\n246: \n247: | ID | Scenario | File |\n248: |---|---|---|\n249: | AI-001 | Deterministic Derived Extraction | [001-derived-extraction.md](auto-indexing/derived-extraction.md) |\n250: | AI-002 | A7 Sanitizer at Every Write Boundary | [002-sanitizer-boundaries.md](auto-indexing/sanitizer-boundaries.md) |\n251: | AI-003 | Provenance Fingerprints and Trust Lanes | [003-provenance-and-trust-lanes.md](auto-indexing/provenance-and-trust-lanes.md) |\n252: | AI-004 | DF/IDF Corpus Stats Active-Only | [004-corpus-df-idf.md](auto-indexing/corpus-df-idf.md) |\n253: | AI-005 | Anti-Stuffing and Cardinality Caps | [005-anti-stuffing.md](auto-indexing/anti-stuffing.md) |\n254: | AI-006 | Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest | [006-doc-frontmatter-harvest.md](auto-indexing/doc-frontmatter-harvest.md) |\n255: | AI-007 | Private Skill Roots Variable | [private-skill-roots.md](auto-indexing/private-skill-roots.md) |\n256: \n257: ---\n258: \n259: ## 13. LIFECYCLE ROUTING\n260: \n261: This category validates lifecycle routing scenarios `LC-001..LC-005`.\n262: \n263: | ID | Scenario | File |\n264: |---|---|---|\n265: | LC-001 | Derived-Lane-Only Age Haircut | [001-age-haircut.md](lifecycle-routing/age-haircut.md) |\n266: | LC-002 | Asymmetric Supersession Redirects | [002-supersession.md](lifecycle-routing/supersession.md) |\n267: | LC-003 | Archive and Future Skills Indexed But Not Routed | [003-archive-handling.md](lifecycle-routing/archive-handling.md) |\n268: | LC-004 | Schema v1 to v2 Additive Backfill | [004-schema-migration.md](lifecycle-routing/schema-migration.md) |\n269: | LC-005 | Lifecycle-Level Rollback | [005-rollback-lifecycle.md](lifecycle-routing/rollback-lifecycle.md) |\n270: \n271: ---\n272: \n273: ## 14. SCORER FUSION\n274: \n275: This category validates scorer fusion scenarios `SC-001..SC-005`.\n276: \n277: | ID | Scenario | File |\n278: |---|---|---|\n279: | SC-001 | Five-Lane Analytical Fusion | [001-five-lane-fusion.md](scorer-fusion/five-lane-fusion.md) |\n280: | SC-002 | Registry Projection Drift Guard and workflowMode Publication | [002-projection.md](scorer-fusion/projection.md) |\n281: | SC-003 | Top-2 Ambiguity Window | [003-ambiguity.md](scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) |\n282: | SC-004 | Lane Contribution Attribution | [004-lane-attribution.md](scorer-fusion/lane-attribution.md) |\n283: | SC-005 | Lane-by-Lane Ablation Protocol | [005-ablation.md](scorer-fusion/ablation.md) |\n284: \n285: ---\n286: \n287: ## 15. PYTHON COMPAT\n288: \n289: This category validates python compat scenarios `PC-001..PC-005`.\n290: \n291: | ID | Scenario | File |\n292: |---|---|---|\n293: | PC-001 | Python Shim --stdin Round-Trip | [001-stdin-mode.md](python-compat/stdin-mode.md) |\n294: | PC-002 | --force-native and --force-local Toggles | [002-force-native-force-local.md](python-compat/force-native-force-local.md) |\n295: | PC-003 | threshold Confidence Flag | [003-threshold-flag.md](python-compat/threshold-flag.md) |\n296: | PC-004 | Python Regression Dataset | [004-regression-suite.md](python-compat/regression-suite.md) |\n297: | PC-005 | Python Bench Runner | [005-bench-runner.md](python-compat/bench-runner.md) |\n298: \n299: ---\n300: \n301: ## 16. DOCTOR COMMAND SCENARIOS\n302: \n303: Doctor command scenarios for the Skill Advisor now live in the [central manual testing playbook](../../../manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md).\n304: \n305: ---\n306: \n307: ## 17. AUTOMATED TEST CROSS-REFERENCE\n308: \n309: The active inventory check lives at `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts`. It verifies the root playbook rows, the live per-feature file inventory and the scenario package count.\n310: \n311: | Area | Automated test anchors |\n312: |---|---|\n313: | Native command surface | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-rebuild.vitest.ts` |\n314: | Hooks and plugin | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/settings-driven-invocation-parity.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/runtime-parity.vitest.ts`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-state.test.cjs`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-continuation.test.cjs` |\n315: | Compatibility and Python entrypoints | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts` |\n316: | Scoring, lifecycle, indexing and daemon state | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` |\n317: \n318: Validator limitation: `validate_document.py` validates this root document and per-feature documents individually, but it does not prove that every command can execute in the current sandbox. Operators must capture execution evidence during release review.\n319: \n320: ---\n321: \n322: ## 18. FEATURE CATALOG CROSS-REFERENCE INDEX\n323: \n324: | Scenario prefix | Category | Feature file directory |\n325: |---|---|---|\n326: | NC | Native command surface | `native-cli-tools/` |\n327: | CL | CLI hooks and plugin | `cli-hooks-and-plugin/` |\n328: | CP | Compat and disable | `compat-and-disable/` |\n329: | OP | Operator H5 | `operator-h5/` |\n330: | AU | Auto update daemon | `auto-update-daemon/` |\n331: | AI | Auto indexing | `auto-indexing/` |\n332: | LC | Lifecycle routing | `lifecycle-routing/` |\n333: | SC | Scorer fusion | `scorer-fusion/` |\n334: | PC | Python compat | `python-compat/` |\n335: \n336: Per-feature files include SOURCE FILES sections with implementation, catalog and test anchors where the source scenario provided them.\n337: \n338: ### 18.5 Catalog group ↔ playbook category mapping (F37 cross-reference)\n339: \n340: The catalog uses 7 groups. The playbook uses 9 categories. Mapping is intentionally asymmetric, not 1:1. This table documents the relationships so operators can navigate between surfaces without surprise.\n341: \n342: | Feature catalog group | Maps to playbook category | Notes |\n343: |---|---|---|\n344: | `daemon-and-freshness` | `auto-update-daemon` (primary) plus `operator-h5` (operator state subset) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: daemon mechanics in 05, operator-facing trust-state scenarios in 04 |\n345: | `auto-indexing` | `auto-indexing` | 1:1 mapping |\n346: | `lifecycle-routing` | `lifecycle-routing` | 1:1 mapping |\n347: | `scorer-fusion` | `scorer-fusion` | 1:1 mapping |\n348: | `cli-surface` | `native-cli-tools` plus `cli-hooks-and-plugin` | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: native command-surface scenarios in 01, CLI hook plus plugin invocation scenarios in 02 |\n349: | `hooks-and-plugin` | `cli-hooks-and-plugin` (primary) plus `compat-and-disable` (rollback states) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: happy-path hook behavior in 02, disable plus rollback flags in 03 |\n350: | `python-compat` | `python-compat` | 1:1 mapping (note gap-09 is intentional per playbook §1) |\n351: | (none) | `compat-and-disable` | Compat plus disable flags cut across multiple catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n352: | (none) | `operator-h5` | H5 operator state scenarios cut across catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n353: \n354: **Why asymmetric**: catalog groups model feature ownership (what the code does), while playbook categories model operator workflow (what an operator validates in one sitting). A single feature group can require multiple operator scenarios that span different runtime contexts (native command surface vs CLI hook vs compat shim). The asymmetry is documented here rather than removed by renumbering because renumbering would break checked-in inventory tests plus deep cross-references in earlier packets.\n355: \n356: ---\n357: \n358: ## 19. LEGACY ID CROSS-REFERENCE\n359: \n360: - SAD-001 -> NC-001 (native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md)\n361: - SAD-002 -> NC-004 (native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md)\n362: - SAD-003 -> CL-001 (cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md)\n363: - SAD-004 -> NC-006 (native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md, lines 1-363\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\"\n3: description: \"Canonical sk-doc manual testing playbook for the Skill Advisor manual scenario corpus across the native command surface, runtime hooks, compatibility, daemon state, indexing, lifecycle, scorer and Python compatibility workflows.\"\n4: version: 0.8.12.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\n8: \n9: This document combines the canonical manual-validation contract for the `skill_advisor` package into a single reference. The root playbook acts as the operator directory, review protocol and orchestration guide while the per-feature files carry scenario-specific execution truth.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: This playbook package follows the sk-doc manual testing playbook template. The current corpus is the historical operator scenario set merged into the canonical manual-testing package on 2026-05-07, with former `SAD-NNN` scenarios absorbed into their current `NC` and `CL` homes.\n14: \n15: Canonical package artifacts:\n16: - `manual-testing-playbook.md`\n17: - `native-cli-tools/`\n18: - `cli-hooks-and-plugin/`\n19: - `compat-and-disable/`\n20: - `operator-h5/`\n21: - `auto-update-daemon/`\n22: - `auto-indexing/`\n23: - `lifecycle-routing/`\n24: - `scorer-fusion/`\n25: - `python-compat/`\n26: \n27: ### Result persistence\n28: \n29: <!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n30: A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome, its reason and its evidence paths are recorded by hand in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. The first recorded run creates that folder, because the wrapper that once wrote these records was retired and no renderer writes them now.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n35: \n36: This playbook provides 53 scenario files across 9 categories, one of them a live conversation scenario, validating the Skill Advisor surface. Scenario IDs use a multi-prefix scheme: `NC` for the native command surface, `CL` for CLI hooks plus plugin behavior, `CP` for compatibility plus disable controls, `OP` for operator H5 states, `AU` for auto-update daemon behavior, `AI` for auto-indexing, `LC` for lifecycle routing, `SC` for scorer fusion and `PC` for Python compatibility.\n37: \n38: > **Numbering note (gap-09).** The directory layout skips slot `09--*` between `scorer-fusion` and `python-compat`. This mirrors the `feature-catalog/` 05-gap pattern and is an intentional historical reservation from initial scaffold design. The gap is preserved to keep spec-folder cross-reference stability across packets. Do not renumber.\n39: \n40: Coverage note (2026-05-07): the prior operator scenario corpus now lives under `manual-testing-playbook/` as the canonical manual validation package. Former `SAD-NNN` IDs are deprecated plus mapped to current feature files in section 18. The source root index describes 42 scenarios. The category tree holds 53 scenario files named by feature, none of them `NNN-*.md`, and still no `CL-002`. This root playbook reflects the live file inventory.\n41: \n42: ### Realistic Test Model\n43: \n44: 1. A realistic user request is given to an operator or runtime.\n45: 2. The operator decides whether to call the native command through the CLI, run a prompt-time hook, execute compatibility shims or inspect daemon/indexing behavior.\n46: 3. The operator captures command transcripts, CLI JSON envelopes, hook stdout/stderr and final verdicts.\n47: 4. The scenario passes only when observed output is prompt-safe, user-visible and consistent with the documented feature contract.\n48: \n49: ### What Each Feature File Should Explain\n50: \n51: - The realistic user request or operational state that should trigger the behavior.\n52: - The deterministic setup and execution steps.\n53: - The expected signals and failure modes.\n54: - The desired user-visible outcome.\n55: - The implementation, automated test or catalog anchors that justify the scenario.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. GLOBAL PRECONDITIONS\n60: \n61: 1. Working directory is the repository root.\n62: 2. Node.js and Python 3 are available.\n63: 3. The advisor runtime build is current:\n64: \n65: ```bash\n66: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 4. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset unless a scenario explicitly sets it.\n70: 5. Operators can run the documented advisor CLI and Node/Python commands from the repo root.\n71: 6. Destructive or stateful scenarios use a disposable workspace copy unless the scenario explicitly validates the live checkout.\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 3. GLOBAL EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n76: \n77: - Scenario ID and per-feature file path.\n78: - User request or operational condition used.\n79: - Exact command transcript or CLI call payload.\n80: - Full JSON output or a focused excerpt containing asserted fields.\n81: - Exit code for shell commands.\n82: - Hook stdout and stderr captured separately when hook scripts are involved.\n83: - Final user-facing verdict with rationale.\n84: - Triage notes for every `FAIL`, `PARTIAL` or `SKIP`.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. DETERMINISTIC COMMAND NOTATION\n89: \n90: - CLI commands shown as `bash: <command>`.\n91: - CLI calls shown as `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command> --format json`.\n92: - Hook stdin payloads shown as JSON piped into the compiled hook script.\n93: - `->` separates sequential steps inside one scenario contract.\n94: - File references use repo-root-relative paths unless otherwise stated.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. REVIEW PROTOCOL AND RELEASE READINESS\n99: \n100: ### Inputs Required\n101: \n102: 1. `manual-testing-playbook.md`.\n103: 2. Referenced per-feature files under `manual-testing-playbook/NN__category_name/`.\n104: 3. Scenario execution evidence.\n105: 4. Feature-to-scenario coverage map.\n106: 5. Triage notes for all non-pass outcomes.\n107: \n108: ### Scenario Acceptance Rules\n109: \n110: For each executed scenario, check:\n111: \n112: 1. Preconditions were satisfied.\n113: 2. Prompt and command sequence were executed as written.\n114: 3. Expected signals are present in the captured output.\n115: 4. Evidence is complete and readable.\n116: 5. Raw prompt text is not leaked into diagnostics, attribution, cache metadata or trust-state fields unless the scenario explicitly validates input echo behavior.\n117: 6. Outcome rationale is explicit and references the user-visible behavior.\n118: \n119: Scenario verdict:\n120: - `PASS`: all acceptance checks true.\n121: - `PARTIAL`: core behavior works but non-critical evidence or metadata is incomplete.\n122: - `FAIL`: expected behavior missing, contradictory output appears, prompt text leaks or a critical check failed.\n123: - `SKIP`: a real sandbox, dependency or runtime blocker prevents execution and is documented.\n124: \n125: ### Feature Verdict Rules\n126: \n127: - `PASS`: all mapped scenarios for the feature are `PASS`.\n128: - `PARTIAL`: at least one mapped scenario is `PARTIAL`, none are `FAIL`.\n129: - `FAIL`: any mapped scenario is `FAIL`.\n130: \n131: ### Release Readiness Rule\n132: \n133: Release is `READY` only when all 53 scenario files are `PASS` or have an approved `SKIP` with a real blocker and no prompt-safety, rebuild, daemon, indexing, lifecycle, scorer or compatibility failure remains unresolved.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. SUB-AGENT ORCHESTRATION AND WAVE PLANNING\n138: \n139: ### Purpose\n140: \n141: This section records wave planning for the canonical Skill Advisor manual test package. The scenario corpus is large enough to split across operators while keeping category ownership clear.\n142: \n143: ### Operational Rules\n144: \n145: 1. Probe build state first with the advisor runtime build command.\n146: 2. Run native command-surface scenarios before hook scenarios so runtime failures can be separated from scorer or command failures.\n147: 3. Run rebuild and daemon scenarios in disposable workspaces when validating repair or generated state.\n148: 4. Assign explicit scenario IDs before parallel execution.\n149: 5. Keep stdout, stderr and CLI JSON evidence in scenario-specific files under `/tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/`.\n150: 6. After each wave, record verdicts and blockers before starting the next wave.\n151: \n152: ### Recommended Wave Plan\n153: \n154: - **Wave 1**: `NC-001..NC-012` native command-surface behavior.\n155: - **Wave 2**: `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-010` runtime hooks, the OpenCode plugin, CLI fallback and the goal plugin.\n156: - **Wave 3**: `CP-001..CP-004` compatibility and disable controls.\n157: - **Wave 4**: `OP-001..OP-003` H5 operator states.\n158: - **Wave 5**: `AU-001..AU-005` auto-update daemon behavior.\n159: - **Wave 6**: `AI-001..AI-007` auto-indexing behavior.\n160: - **Wave 7**: `LC-001..LC-005` lifecycle routing.\n161: - **Wave 8**: `SC-001..SC-005` scorer fusion.\n162: - **Wave 9**: `PC-001..PC-005` Python compatibility.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. NATIVE COMMAND SURFACE\n167: \n168: This category validates native command-surface scenarios `NC-001..NC-012`.\n169: \n170: | ID | Scenario | File |\n171: |---|---|---|\n172: | NC-001 | Native advisor_recommend Happy Path | [native-recommend-happy-path.md](native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md) |\n173: | NC-002 | Native advisor_status Transitions | [native-status-transitions.md](native-cli-tools/native-status-transitions.md) |\n174: | NC-003 | Native advisor_validate Slice Bundle | [native-validate-slices.md](native-cli-tools/native-validate-slices.md) |\n175: | NC-004 | Ambiguous Brief Rendering | [ambiguous-brief-rendering.md](native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md) |\n176: | NC-005 | Lifecycle Redirect Metadata | [lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md](native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md) |\n177: | NC-006 | Advisor Status and Rebuild Separation | [advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md](native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md) |\n178: | NC-007 | Skill Graph Status | [skill-graph-status.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-status.md) |\n179: | NC-008 | Skill Graph Query | [skill-graph-query.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-query.md) |\n180: | NC-009 | Skill Graph Validate | [skill-graph-validate.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-validate.md) |\n181: | NC-010 | Shadow-Delta Sink Opt-In | [shadow-delta-sink.md](native-cli-tools/shadow-delta-sink.md) |\n182: | NC-011 | Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command | [hub-command-attachment.md](native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md) |\n183: | NC-012 | A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered | [command-offer-fit-gates.md](native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md) |\n184: \n185: ---\n186: \n187: ## 8. CLI HOOKS AND PLUGIN\n188: \n189: This category validates cli hooks and plugin scenarios `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-010`.\n190: \n191: | ID | Scenario | File |\n192: |---|---|---|\n193: | CL-001 | Claude Code UserPromptSubmit Hook | [001-claude-user-prompt-submit.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md) |\n194: | CL-004 | OpenCode Native Hooks And Wrapper Fallback | 004-opencode-hook-and-wrapper.md (not yet authored) |\n195: | CL-005 | OpenCode Plugin | [005-opencode-plugin-bridge.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/opencode-plugin-bridge.md) |\n196: | CL-006 | skill-advisor CLI Fallback Surface (028) | [006-skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md) |\n197: | CL-007 | Goal OpenCode Plugin | [goal-opencode-plugin.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) |\n198: | CL-008 | Goal OpenCode Later-Turn Injection | [goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md) |\n199: | CL-009 | The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session | [brief-command-policy-word.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md) |\n200: | CL-010 | The Vibe Brief Names Each Command's Document | [vibe-command-documents.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/vibe-command-documents.md) |\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## 9. COMPAT AND DISABLE\n205: \n206: This category validates compat and disable scenarios `CP-001..CP-004`.\n207: \n208: | ID | Scenario | File |\n209: |---|---|---|\n210: | CP-001 | Python Shim --stdin Mode | [001-python-shim-stdin.md](compat-and-disable/python-shim-stdin.md) |\n211: | CP-002 | Force Local And Force Native Toggles | [002-force-local-force-native.md](compat-and-disable/force-local-force-native.md) |\n212: | CP-003 | Global Disable Flag | [003-global-disable-flag.md](compat-and-disable/global-disable-flag.md) |\n213: | CP-004 | Daemon Absent Fallback | [004-daemon-absent-fallback.md](compat-and-disable/daemon-absent-fallback.md) |\n214: \n215: ---\n216: \n217: ## 10. OPERATOR H5\n218: \n219: This category validates operator h5 scenarios `OP-001..OP-003`.\n220: \n221: | ID | Scenario | File |\n222: |---|---|---|\n223: | OP-001 | Degraded Daemon | [001-degraded-daemon.md](operator-h5/degraded-daemon.md) |\n224: | OP-002 | Quarantined Daemon | [002-quarantined-daemon.md](operator-h5/quarantined-daemon.md) |\n225: | OP-003 | Unavailable Daemon | [003-unavailable-daemon.md](operator-h5/unavailable-daemon.md) |\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 11. AUTO UPDATE DAEMON\n230: \n231: This category validates auto update daemon scenarios `AU-001..AU-005`.\n232: \n233: | ID | Scenario | File |\n234: |---|---|---|\n235: | AU-001 | Chokidar Watcher Narrow Scope | [001-watcher-narrow-scope.md](auto-update-daemon/watcher-narrow-scope.md) |\n236: | AU-002 | Workspace Single-Writer Lease | [002-lease-single-writer.md](auto-update-daemon/lease-single-writer.md) |\n237: | AU-003 | Daemon Lifecycle and SIGTERM | [003-daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md](auto-update-daemon/daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md) |\n238: | AU-004 | Generation-Tagged Snapshot Publication | [004-generation-publication.md](auto-update-daemon/generation-publication.md) |\n239: | AU-005 | Rebuild From Source on Corrupt SQLite | [005-rebuild-from-source.md](auto-update-daemon/rebuild-from-source.md) |\n240: \n241: ---\n242: \n243: ## 12. AUTO INDEXING\n244: \n245: This category validates auto indexing scenarios `AI-001..AI-007`.\n246: \n247: | ID | Scenario | File |\n248: |---|---|---|\n249: | AI-001 | Deterministic Derived Extraction | [001-derived-extraction.md](auto-indexing/derived-extraction.md) |\n250: | AI-002 | A7 Sanitizer at Every Write Boundary | [002-sanitizer-boundaries.md](auto-indexing/sanitizer-boundaries.md) |\n251: | AI-003 | Provenance Fingerprints and Trust Lanes | [003-provenance-and-trust-lanes.md](auto-indexing/provenance-and-trust-lanes.md) |\n252: | AI-004 | DF/IDF Corpus Stats Active-Only | [004-corpus-df-idf.md](auto-indexing/corpus-df-idf.md) |\n253: | AI-005 | Anti-Stuffing and Cardinality Caps | [005-anti-stuffing.md](auto-indexing/anti-stuffing.md) |\n254: | AI-006 | Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest | [006-doc-frontmatter-harvest.md](auto-indexing/doc-frontmatter-harvest.md) |\n255: | AI-007 | Private Skill Roots Variable | [private-skill-roots.md](auto-indexing/private-skill-roots.md) |\n256: \n257: ---\n258: \n259: ## 13. LIFECYCLE ROUTING\n260: \n261: This category validates lifecycle routing scenarios `LC-001..LC-005`.\n262: \n263: | ID | Scenario | File |\n264: |---|---|---|\n265: | LC-001 | Derived-Lane-Only Age Haircut | [001-age-haircut.md](lifecycle-routing/age-haircut.md) |\n266: | LC-002 | Asymmetric Supersession Redirects | [002-supersession.md](lifecycle-routing/supersession.md) |\n267: | LC-003 | Archive and Future Skills Indexed But Not Routed | [003-archive-handling.md](lifecycle-routing/archive-handling.md) |\n268: | LC-004 | Schema v1 to v2 Additive Backfill | [004-schema-migration.md](lifecycle-routing/schema-migration.md) |\n269: | LC-005 | Lifecycle-Level Rollback | [005-rollback-lifecycle.md](lifecycle-routing/rollback-lifecycle.md) |\n270: \n271: ---\n272: \n273: ## 14. SCORER FUSION\n274: \n275: This category validates scorer fusion scenarios `SC-001..SC-005`.\n276: \n277: | ID | Scenario | File |\n278: |---|---|---|\n279: | SC-001 | Five-Lane Analytical Fusion | [001-five-lane-fusion.md](scorer-fusion/five-lane-fusion.md) |\n280: | SC-002 | Registry Projection Drift Guard and workflowMode Publication | [002-projection.md](scorer-fusion/projection.md) |\n281: | SC-003 | Top-2 Ambiguity Window | [003-ambiguity.md](scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) |\n282: | SC-004 | Lane Contribution Attribution | [004-lane-attribution.md](scorer-fusion/lane-attribution.md) |\n283: | SC-005 | Lane-by-Lane Ablation Protocol | [005-ablation.md](scorer-fusion/ablation.md) |\n284: \n285: ---\n286: \n287: ## 15. PYTHON COMPAT\n288: \n289: This category validates python compat scenarios `PC-001..PC-005`.\n290: \n291: | ID | Scenario | File |\n292: |---|---|---|\n293: | PC-001 | Python Shim --stdin Round-Trip | [001-stdin-mode.md](python-compat/stdin-mode.md) |\n294: | PC-002 | --force-native and --force-local Toggles | [002-force-native-force-local.md](python-compat/force-native-force-local.md) |\n295: | PC-003 | threshold Confidence Flag | [003-threshold-flag.md](python-compat/threshold-flag.md) |\n296: | PC-004 | Python Regression Dataset | [004-regression-suite.md](python-compat/regression-suite.md) |\n297: | PC-005 | Python Bench Runner | [005-bench-runner.md](python-compat/bench-runner.md) |\n298: \n299: ---\n300: \n301: ## 16. DOCTOR COMMAND SCENARIOS\n302: \n303: Doctor command scenarios for the Skill Advisor now live in the [central manual testing playbook](../../../manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md).\n304: \n305: ---\n306: \n307: ## 17. AUTOMATED TEST CROSS-REFERENCE\n308: \n309: The active inventory check lives at `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts`. It verifies the root playbook rows, the live per-feature file inventory and the scenario package count.\n310: \n311: | Area | Automated test anchors |\n312: |---|---|\n313: | Native command surface | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-rebuild.vitest.ts` |\n314: | Hooks and plugin | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/settings-driven-invocation-parity.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/runtime-parity.vitest.ts`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-state.test.cjs`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-continuation.test.cjs` |\n315: | Compatibility and Python entrypoints | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts` |\n316: | Scoring, lifecycle, indexing and daemon state | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` |\n317: \n318: Validator limitation: `validate_document.py` validates this root document and per-feature documents individually, but it does not prove that every command can execute in the current sandbox. Operators must capture execution evidence during release review.\n319: \n320: ---\n321: \n322: ## 18. FEATURE CATALOG CROSS-REFERENCE INDEX\n323: \n324: | Scenario prefix | Category | Feature file directory |\n325: |---|---|---|\n326: | NC | Native command surface | `native-cli-tools/` |\n327: | CL | CLI hooks and plugin | `cli-hooks-and-plugin/` |\n328: | CP | Compat and disable | `compat-and-disable/` |\n329: | OP | Operator H5 | `operator-h5/` |\n330: | AU | Auto update daemon | `auto-update-daemon/` |\n331: | AI | Auto indexing | `auto-indexing/` |\n332: | LC | Lifecycle routing | `lifecycle-routing/` |\n333: | SC | Scorer fusion | `scorer-fusion/` |\n334: | PC | Python compat | `python-compat/` |\n335: \n336: Per-feature files include SOURCE FILES sections with implementation, catalog and test anchors where the source scenario provided them.\n337: \n338: ### 18.5 Catalog group ↔ playbook category mapping (F37 cross-reference)\n339: \n340: The catalog uses 7 groups. The playbook uses 9 categories. Mapping is intentionally asymmetric, not 1:1. This table documents the relationships so operators can navigate between surfaces without surprise.\n341: \n342: | Feature catalog group | Maps to playbook category | Notes |\n343: |---|---|---|\n344: | `daemon-and-freshness` | `auto-update-daemon` (primary) plus `operator-h5` (operator state subset) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: daemon mechanics in 05, operator-facing trust-state scenarios in 04 |\n345: | `auto-indexing` | `auto-indexing` | 1:1 mapping |\n346: | `lifecycle-routing` | `lifecycle-routing` | 1:1 mapping |\n347: | `scorer-fusion` | `scorer-fusion` | 1:1 mapping |\n348: | `cli-surface` | `native-cli-tools` plus `cli-hooks-and-plugin` | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: native command-surface scenarios in 01, CLI hook plus plugin invocation scenarios in 02 |\n349: | `hooks-and-plugin` | `cli-hooks-and-plugin` (primary) plus `compat-and-disable` (rollback states) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: happy-path hook behavior in 02, disable plus rollback flags in 03 |\n350: | `python-compat` | `python-compat` | 1:1 mapping (note gap-09 is intentional per playbook §1) |\n351: | (none) | `compat-and-disable` | Compat plus disable flags cut across multiple catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n352: | (none) | `operator-h5` | H5 operator state scenarios cut across catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n353: \n354: **Why asymmetric**: catalog groups model feature ownership (what the code does), while playbook categories model operator workflow (what an operator validates in one sitting). A single feature group can require multiple operator scenarios that span different runtime contexts (native command surface vs CLI hook vs compat shim). The asymmetry is documented here rather than removed by renumbering because renumbering would break checked-in inventory tests plus deep cross-references in earlier packets.\n355: \n356: ---\n357: \n358: ## 19. LEGACY ID CROSS-REFERENCE\n359: \n360: - SAD-001 -> NC-001 (native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md)\n361: - SAD-002 -> NC-004 (native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md)\n362: - SAD-003 -> CL-001 (cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md)\n363: - SAD-004 -> NC-006 (native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760694110,"end":1794760694952}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760694956,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e012db110018RL4KNSf5KTNO5","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e012a9e1001eZsPxCfv3XFVDL","type":"tool","id":"call_17fce49163d84e998b06722f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: \"NC-012 A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered\"\n3: description: \"Manual validation that advisor_recommend withholds a hub's slash command when the prompt refuses it, asks about it, rules out a change or asks for a small correction, and still names a command row's own command with its policy.\"\n4: version: 1.0.1.0\n5: id: NC-012\n6: category: native_cli_tools\n7: stage: routing\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # NC-012 A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered\n11: \n12: Prompt: Manual validation that advisor_recommend withholds a hub's slash command when the prompt refuses it, asks about it, rules out a change or asks for a small correction, and still names a command row's own command with its policy.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: A hub route names the command that acts on the mode it picked. That offer is only right when the reader asked for the workflow. A prompt that names the command to refuse it, asks a question about it, rules out a change or asks for a correction inside one existing document is not asking for the workflow, so the offer is withheld.\n19: \n20: Three offer gates stand between a route and its command:\n21: \n22: 1. A command the prompt refuses or asks about is a naming, not a request.\n23: 2. A prompt that rules out a change gets no command from either path.\n24: 3. A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow.\n25: \n26: This scenario checks those gates with five real prompts and adds one prompt whose recommendation row is a command itself. A command row carries its own command and policy, so the reader sees `/doctor:git setup` with `run` even though the leading skill in that result names no command.\n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n31: \n32: - Objective: Confirm the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates withhold a hub command while a plain request still carries `/create:readme` with `start`, and read a command row's own policy.\n33: - Real user request: `When I ask what /create:readme does, why does the advisor keep offering to run it?`\n34: - Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.`\n35: - Expected execution process: Run the six commands below from the repository root and read the fields named under Expected.\n36: - Expected signals: no command on the refusal, the question, the no-edit request and the typo fix, `/create:readme` with `start` on the plain request, and `/doctor:git setup` with `run` on the git prompt's command row.\n37: - Desired user-visible outcome: The reader is offered a workflow only when they asked for it, and a command row names the policy that says whether it runs or asks.\n38: - Pass/fail: PASS if all six results match the expected signals. FAIL if any withheld command appears, a readme prompt loses its single route to `sk-create-readme`, the plain request loses its command, or the git command row carries a different command or policy.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n43: \n44: ### Prompt\n45: \n46: - Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.`\n47: \n48: ### Commands\n49: \n50: 1. `bash: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook`\n51: 2. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"do not run /create:readme, just tell me what it covers\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-refusal.json`\n52: 3. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"what does /create:readme do\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-question.json`\n53: 4. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"write a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-plain.json`\n54: 5. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"write a readme for the utils folder, no edits yet\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-no-edit.json`\n55: 6. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"fix the typo on line 12 of the scripts readme\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-typo.json`\n56: 7. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-git.json`\n57: 8. In each file read `status` and, for each entry in `data.recommendations`, the `skillId`, `score`, `confidence`, `compiledRoute.targets[0].workflowMode`, `command` and `policy`.\n58: \n59: ### Exact Prompt Table\n60: \n61: | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n62: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n63: | NC-012 | Command the prompt does not ask for is not offered | Confirm the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates withhold a hub command while a plain request still carries its command | `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.` | Commands 1 to 8 above | No command on the refusal, the question, the no-edit request and the typo fix. `/create:readme` with `start` on the plain request. `/doctor:git setup` with `run` on the git command row. A single route to `sk-create-readme` on the five readme prompts | The six JSON files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all six results match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n64: \n65: ### Expected\n66: \n67: - Every envelope has `status: \"ok\"` and the shell exit code is `0`.\n68: - `nc-012-refusal.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `do not run` is a decline lead before the command.\n69: - `nc-012-question.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `what does` is an ask lead before the command.\n70: - `nc-012-plain.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It carries `command: \"/create:readme\"` and `policy: \"start\"`.\n71: - `nc-012-no-edit.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `no edits yet` rules out a change.\n72: - `nc-012-typo.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `typo` and `line 12` mark a correction inside one document rather than a request for a new one.\n73: - `nc-012-git.json`: `sk-git` ranks first and carries no `command`. The `command-doctor-git-setup` row ranks second and carries `command: \"/doctor:git setup\"` with `policy: \"run\"`.\n74: \n75: ### Evidence\n76: \n77: Keep the six JSON files and the terminal transcript. Reduced to one line per checked recommendation, a passing run reads like this:\n78: \n79: ```text\n80: do not run /create:readme, just tell me what it covers\n81:   status=ok\n82:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n83: what does /create:readme do\n84:   status=ok\n85:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n86: write a readme for the utils folder\n87:   status=ok\n88:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=/create:readme policy=start\n89: write a readme for the utils folder, no edits yet\n90:   status=ok\n91:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n92: fix the typo on line 12 of the scripts readme\n93:   status=ok\n94:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n95: Check my git hooks setup\n96:   status=ok\n97:   sk-git command=(absent)\n98:   command-doctor-git-setup command=/doctor:git setup policy=run\n99: ```\n100: \n101: The scores and confidences can move after an index rebuild. The routes, the commands and the policy words are the checked signals.\n102: \n103: ### Pass / Fail\n104: \n105: - **Pass**: all six results match Expected.\n106: - **Fail**: a refusal, a question, a no-edit request or a typo fix carries a command, the plain request carries no command or a different policy, or the git command row carries a different command or policy.\n107: \n108: ### Failure Triage\n109: \n110: 1. A refusal or question still carries a command: compare the prompt with `COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS` and `COMMAND_ASK_LEADS` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, then run `runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`.\n111: 2. A no-edit request still carries a command: check `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` and `isNoEditRequest()`, then run the same test file.\n112: 3. A typo fix still carries a command: check `SMALL_EDIT_WORDING`, `CORRECTION_VERB` and `SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS` beside each other.\n113: 4. The plain request carries no command: check the `sk-doc` entry for `sk-create-readme` in `MODE_COMMANDS`, and confirm the runtime build is current.\n114: 5. The git command row carries a different command or policy: check the `/doctor:git setup` entry in `TRACKED_COMMANDS`.\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## 4. SOURCE FILES\n119: \n120: | File | Role |\n121: |---|---|\n122: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | `commandOnlyMentioned`, `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` and `SMALL_EDIT_WORDING`, plus the mode table that maps `sk-create-readme` to `/create:readme` |\n123: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates |\n124: | [`advisor-recommend.md`](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md) | Feature catalog entry for `advisor_recommend` |\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n129: \n130: - Group: Native Command Surface\n131: - Playbook ID: NC-012\n132: - Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n133: - Feature file path: native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: \"NC-012 A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered\"\n3: description: \"Manual validation that advisor_recommend withholds a hub's slash command when the prompt refuses it, asks about it, rules out a change or asks for a small correction, and still names a command row's own command with its policy.\"\n4: version: 1.0.1.0\n5: id: NC-012\n6: category: native_cli_tools\n7: stage: routing\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # NC-012 A Command the Prompt Does Not Ask For Is Not Offered\n11: \n12: Prompt: Manual validation that advisor_recommend withholds a hub's slash command when the prompt refuses it, asks about it, rules out a change or asks for a small correction, and still names a command row's own command with its policy.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: A hub route names the command that acts on the mode it picked. That offer is only right when the reader asked for the workflow. A prompt that names the command to refuse it, asks a question about it, rules out a change or asks for a correction inside one existing document is not asking for the workflow, so the offer is withheld.\n19: \n20: Three offer gates stand between a route and its command:\n21: \n22: 1. A command the prompt refuses or asks about is a naming, not a request.\n23: 2. A prompt that rules out a change gets no command from either path.\n24: 3. A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow.\n25: \n26: This scenario checks those gates with five real prompts and adds one prompt whose recommendation row is a command itself. A command row carries its own command and policy, so the reader sees `/doctor:git setup` with `run` even though the leading skill in that result names no command.\n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n31: \n32: - Objective: Confirm the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates withhold a hub command while a plain request still carries `/create:readme` with `start`, and read a command row's own policy.\n33: - Real user request: `When I ask what /create:readme does, why does the advisor keep offering to run it?`\n34: - Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.`\n35: - Expected execution process: Run the six commands below from the repository root and read the fields named under Expected.\n36: - Expected signals: no command on the refusal, the question, the no-edit request and the typo fix, `/create:readme` with `start` on the plain request, and `/doctor:git setup` with `run` on the git prompt's command row.\n37: - Desired user-visible outcome: The reader is offered a workflow only when they asked for it, and a command row names the policy that says whether it runs or asks.\n38: - Pass/fail: PASS if all six results match the expected signals. FAIL if any withheld command appears, a readme prompt loses its single route to `sk-create-readme`, the plain request loses its command, or the git command row carries a different command or policy.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n43: \n44: ### Prompt\n45: \n46: - Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.`\n47: \n48: ### Commands\n49: \n50: 1. `bash: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook`\n51: 2. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"do not run /create:readme, just tell me what it covers\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-refusal.json`\n52: 3. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"what does /create:readme do\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-question.json`\n53: 4. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"write a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-plain.json`\n54: 5. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"write a readme for the utils folder, no edits yet\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-no-edit.json`\n55: 6. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"fix the typo on line 12 of the scripts readme\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-typo.json`\n56: 7. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-012-git.json`\n57: 8. In each file read `status` and, for each entry in `data.recommendations`, the `skillId`, `score`, `confidence`, `compiledRoute.targets[0].workflowMode`, `command` and `policy`.\n58: \n59: ### Exact Prompt Table\n60: \n61: | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n62: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n63: | NC-012 | Command the prompt does not ask for is not offered | Confirm the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates withhold a hub command while a plain request still carries its command | `Run advisor_recommend on six prompts and check which recommendations carry a command and its policy word.` | Commands 1 to 8 above | No command on the refusal, the question, the no-edit request and the typo fix. `/create:readme` with `start` on the plain request. `/doctor:git setup` with `run` on the git command row. A single route to `sk-create-readme` on the five readme prompts | The six JSON files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all six results match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n64: \n65: ### Expected\n66: \n67: - Every envelope has `status: \"ok\"` and the shell exit code is `0`.\n68: - `nc-012-refusal.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `do not run` is a decline lead before the command.\n69: - `nc-012-question.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `what does` is an ask lead before the command.\n70: - `nc-012-plain.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It carries `command: \"/create:readme\"` and `policy: \"start\"`.\n71: - `nc-012-no-edit.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `no edits yet` rules out a change.\n72: - `nc-012-typo.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc` with a single route to `sk-create-readme`. It has no `command` key, because `typo` and `line 12` mark a correction inside one document rather than a request for a new one.\n73: - `nc-012-git.json`: `sk-git` ranks first and carries no `command`. The `command-doctor-git-setup` row ranks second and carries `command: \"/doctor:git setup\"` with `policy: \"run\"`.\n74: \n75: ### Evidence\n76: \n77: Keep the six JSON files and the terminal transcript. Reduced to one line per checked recommendation, a passing run reads like this:\n78: \n79: ```text\n80: do not run /create:readme, just tell me what it covers\n81:   status=ok\n82:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n83: what does /create:readme do\n84:   status=ok\n85:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n86: write a readme for the utils folder\n87:   status=ok\n88:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=/create:readme policy=start\n89: write a readme for the utils folder, no edits yet\n90:   status=ok\n91:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n92: fix the typo on line 12 of the scripts readme\n93:   status=ok\n94:   sk-doc route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=(absent)\n95: Check my git hooks setup\n96:   status=ok\n97:   sk-git command=(absent)\n98:   command-doctor-git-setup command=/doctor:git setup policy=run\n99: ```\n100: \n101: The scores and confidences can move after an index rebuild. The routes, the commands and the policy words are the checked signals.\n102: \n103: ### Pass / Fail\n104: \n105: - **Pass**: all six results match Expected.\n106: - **Fail**: a refusal, a question, a no-edit request or a typo fix carries a command, the plain request carries no command or a different policy, or the git command row carries a different command or policy.\n107: \n108: ### Failure Triage\n109: \n110: 1. A refusal or question still carries a command: compare the prompt with `COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS` and `COMMAND_ASK_LEADS` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, then run `runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`.\n111: 2. A no-edit request still carries a command: check `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` and `isNoEditRequest()`, then run the same test file.\n112: 3. A typo fix still carries a command: check `SMALL_EDIT_WORDING`, `CORRECTION_VERB` and `SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS` beside each other.\n113: 4. The plain request carries no command: check the `sk-doc` entry for `sk-create-readme` in `MODE_COMMANDS`, and confirm the runtime build is current.\n114: 5. The git command row carries a different command or policy: check the `/doctor:git setup` entry in `TRACKED_COMMANDS`.\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## 4. SOURCE FILES\n119: \n120: | File | Role |\n121: |---|---|\n122: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | `commandOnlyMentioned`, `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` and `SMALL_EDIT_WORDING`, plus the mode table that maps `sk-create-readme` to `/create:readme` |\n123: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for the refusal, question, no-edit and small-edit gates |\n124: | [`advisor-recommend.md`](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md) | Feature catalog entry for `advisor_recommend` |\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n129: \n130: - Group: Native Command Surface\n131: - Playbook ID: NC-012\n132: - Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n133: - Feature file path: native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760694545,"end":1794760694956}}}}
