{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760708261,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0130cf2001Ub9cSeO7LeWrWe","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e012e68a001VFallgNABGyp5g","type":"tool","id":"call_6445f42c7a484bea9650e824","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md, lines 1-198\n1: ---\n2: title: \"CL-009 The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session\"\n3: description: \"Manual validation that the Claude prompt hook keeps a command's policy word in a parent session and replaces it with the parent-named-only marker in a dispatched child session, for a head command and for a companion command.\"\n4: version: 1.0.1.0\n5: id: CL-009\n6: category: cli_hooks_and_plugin\n7: stage: routing\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # CL-009 The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session\n11: \n12: Prompt: Manual validation that the Claude prompt hook keeps a command's policy word in a parent session and replaces it with the parent-named-only marker in a dispatched child session, for a head command and for a companion command.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: The brief prints a command with the policy that governs it: `run`, `start` or `ask`. A dispatched child cannot ask its operator whether to start an interactive run or one that writes, so the same label swaps only the policy word for `parent-named only` there. The command and its owner stay visible, because the child still has to report what the parent named.\n19: \n20: That swap works in two positions on the brief. The head can carry the command, as `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask)`. A companion line can carry it, as `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)`. A read-only `run` command is the exception, because a child may run it, so its word stays `run` in both sessions.\n21: \n22: This scenario checks three prompts across a parent session and a child session and pins the policy word alone. Scores and the freshness word move with the index and the cache, so they are read, never asserted.\n23: \n24: ---\n25: \n26: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n27: \n28: - Objective: Confirm the head label and the companion label keep their command and owner while the policy word becomes `parent-named only` in a child session, and confirm a read-only `run` companion keeps `run` there.\n29: - Real user request: `Why does the brief tell my subagent that a command is parent-named only when it tells me to run it?`\n30: - Prompt: `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.`\n31: - Expected execution process: Run the six hook calls below from the repository root, each in its own temporary folder, and read the first two lines of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`.\n32: - Expected signals: `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in both sessions, `/doctor:update (ask)` in the parent and `/doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child, and `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child.\n33: - Desired user-visible outcome: The reader sees the policy that governs a command in their own session, and a dispatched child sees the same command marked as one only its parent may name.\n34: - Pass/fail: PASS if all six runs match the expected policy words. FAIL if a word differs, if a command or its owner changes, or if a child run drops the command instead of marking it.\n35: - Advisor runtime build is current.\n36: - The Claude hook script exists at `runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n37: - `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset.\n38: \n39: ---\n40: \n41: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n42: \n43: ### Prompt\n44: \n45: - Prompt: `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.`\n46: \n47: ### Commands\n48: \n49: 1. Create the output folder.\n50: \n51: ```bash\n52: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook\n53: ```\n54: \n55: 2. Run `Check my git hooks setup` in a parent session.\n56: \n57: ```bash\n58: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n59: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-git-parent\"}' \\\n60:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n61:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n62:       2>/dev/null \\\n63:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n64:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-parent.txt\n65: rm -rf \"$T\"\n66: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-parent.txt\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 3. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n70: \n71: ```bash\n72: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n73: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-git-child\"}' \\\n74:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n75:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n76:       2>/dev/null \\\n77:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n78:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-child.txt\n79: rm -rf \"$T\"\n80: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-child.txt\n81: ```\n82: \n83: 4. Run `update skilled to the latest version` in a parent session.\n84: \n85: ```bash\n86: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n87: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update skilled to the latest version\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-update-parent\"}' \\\n88:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n89:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n90:       2>/dev/null \\\n91:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n92:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-parent.txt\n93: rm -rf \"$T\"\n94: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-parent.txt\n95: ```\n96: \n97: 5. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n98: \n99: ```bash\n100: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n101: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update skilled to the latest version\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-update-child\"}' \\\n102:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n103:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n104:       2>/dev/null \\\n105:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n106:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-child.txt\n107: rm -rf \"$T\"\n108: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-child.txt\n109: ```\n110: \n111: 6. Run `spec search is broken even for indexed terms` in a parent session.\n112: \n113: ```bash\n114: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n115: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"spec search is broken even for indexed terms\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-speckit-parent\"}' \\\n116:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n117:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n118:       2>/dev/null \\\n119:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n120:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-parent.txt\n121: rm -rf \"$T\"\n122: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-parent.txt\n123: ```\n124: \n125: 7. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n126: \n127: ```bash\n128: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n129: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"spec search is broken even for indexed terms\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-speckit-child\"}' \\\n130:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n131:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n132:       2>/dev/null \\\n133:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n134:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-child.txt\n135: rm -rf \"$T\"\n136: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-child.txt\n137: ```\n138: \n139: ### Exact Prompt Table\n140: \n141: | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n142: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n143: | CL-009 | Brief command policy word in a parent and a child session | Confirm the policy word stays in a parent session and becomes parent-named only in a child session for a head command and for a companion command | `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.` | Commands 1 to 7 above | `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in both sessions, `/doctor:update (ask)` in the parent and `/doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child, `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child | The six two-line files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all six runs match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n144: \n145: ### Expected\n146: \n147: - `Check my git hooks setup` prints `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in the parent session and in the child session, because a read-only `run` command stays runnable in a child.\n148: - `update skilled to the latest version` prints `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask)` in the parent session and `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child session.\n149: - `spec search is broken even for indexed terms` prints `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent session and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child session.\n150: - Scores, confidences and the freshness word are read but not asserted, because they move with the index and the cache.\n151: \n152: ### Evidence\n153: \n154: Keep the six two-line files and the terminal transcript. A passing run reads like this:\n155: \n156: ```text\n157: Check my git hooks setup\n158:   parent: Also: /doctor:git setup (run)\n159:   child:  Also: /doctor:git setup (run)\n160: update skilled to the latest version\n161:   parent: Advisor: ... use system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask) ... pass.\n162:   child:  Advisor: ... use system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (parent-named only) ... pass.\n163: spec search is broken even for indexed terms\n164:   parent: Also: /doctor:speckit (run)\n165:   child:  Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)\n166: ```\n167: \n168: ### Pass / Fail\n169: \n170: - **Pass**: all six policy words match Expected.\n171: - **Fail**: a policy word differs, a command or its owner changes, or a child run drops the command instead of marking it.\n172: \n173: ### Failure Triage\n174: \n175: 1. A child run keeps `ask`, `start` or a mutating `run` word: check `policyWord()` and `parentNamedOnly()` in `runtime/lib/render.ts`, then run `runtime/tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts`.\n176: 2. A companion line drops its marker: check `companionCommandLabel()` and the companion selection in `renderAdvisorBrief()`, then run `runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts`.\n177: 3. The child run matches the parent for the head command: confirm `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` sits in the environment of the `node` process, not of the shell that builds the payload.\n178: 4. The hook prints `{}` or a stale build warning: rebuild the advisor runtime with `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` and rerun the run.\n179: 5. A command or its owner changes between the two sessions: rebuild the advisor runtime and rerun, because both sessions read the same generated row.\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 4. SOURCE FILES\n184: \n185: | File | Role |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `policyWord` and `companionCommandLabel`, the two functions that choose the policy word and the child marker |\n188: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for the head command and the companion line, including the child marker |\n189: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for every policy and class pair in a child session and in a parent session |\n190: \n191: ---\n192: \n193: ## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n194: \n195: - Group: CLI Hooks And Plugin\n196: - Playbook ID: CL-009\n197: - Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n198: - Feature file path: cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md, lines 1-198\n1: ---\n2: title: \"CL-009 The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session\"\n3: description: \"Manual validation that the Claude prompt hook keeps a command's policy word in a parent session and replaces it with the parent-named-only marker in a dispatched child session, for a head command and for a companion command.\"\n4: version: 1.0.1.0\n5: id: CL-009\n6: category: cli_hooks_and_plugin\n7: stage: routing\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # CL-009 The Brief's Command Policy Word in a Parent and a Child Session\n11: \n12: Prompt: Manual validation that the Claude prompt hook keeps a command's policy word in a parent session and replaces it with the parent-named-only marker in a dispatched child session, for a head command and for a companion command.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: The brief prints a command with the policy that governs it: `run`, `start` or `ask`. A dispatched child cannot ask its operator whether to start an interactive run or one that writes, so the same label swaps only the policy word for `parent-named only` there. The command and its owner stay visible, because the child still has to report what the parent named.\n19: \n20: That swap works in two positions on the brief. The head can carry the command, as `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask)`. A companion line can carry it, as `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)`. A read-only `run` command is the exception, because a child may run it, so its word stays `run` in both sessions.\n21: \n22: This scenario checks three prompts across a parent session and a child session and pins the policy word alone. Scores and the freshness word move with the index and the cache, so they are read, never asserted.\n23: \n24: ---\n25: \n26: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n27: \n28: - Objective: Confirm the head label and the companion label keep their command and owner while the policy word becomes `parent-named only` in a child session, and confirm a read-only `run` companion keeps `run` there.\n29: - Real user request: `Why does the brief tell my subagent that a command is parent-named only when it tells me to run it?`\n30: - Prompt: `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.`\n31: - Expected execution process: Run the six hook calls below from the repository root, each in its own temporary folder, and read the first two lines of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`.\n32: - Expected signals: `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in both sessions, `/doctor:update (ask)` in the parent and `/doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child, and `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child.\n33: - Desired user-visible outcome: The reader sees the policy that governs a command in their own session, and a dispatched child sees the same command marked as one only its parent may name.\n34: - Pass/fail: PASS if all six runs match the expected policy words. FAIL if a word differs, if a command or its owner changes, or if a child run drops the command instead of marking it.\n35: - Advisor runtime build is current.\n36: - The Claude hook script exists at `runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n37: - `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset.\n38: \n39: ---\n40: \n41: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n42: \n43: ### Prompt\n44: \n45: - Prompt: `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.`\n46: \n47: ### Commands\n48: \n49: 1. Create the output folder.\n50: \n51: ```bash\n52: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook\n53: ```\n54: \n55: 2. Run `Check my git hooks setup` in a parent session.\n56: \n57: ```bash\n58: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n59: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-git-parent\"}' \\\n60:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n61:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n62:       2>/dev/null \\\n63:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n64:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-parent.txt\n65: rm -rf \"$T\"\n66: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-parent.txt\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 3. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n70: \n71: ```bash\n72: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n73: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"Check my git hooks setup\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-git-child\"}' \\\n74:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n75:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n76:       2>/dev/null \\\n77:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n78:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-child.txt\n79: rm -rf \"$T\"\n80: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-git-child.txt\n81: ```\n82: \n83: 4. Run `update skilled to the latest version` in a parent session.\n84: \n85: ```bash\n86: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n87: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update skilled to the latest version\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-update-parent\"}' \\\n88:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n89:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n90:       2>/dev/null \\\n91:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n92:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-parent.txt\n93: rm -rf \"$T\"\n94: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-parent.txt\n95: ```\n96: \n97: 5. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n98: \n99: ```bash\n100: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n101: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update skilled to the latest version\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-update-child\"}' \\\n102:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n103:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n104:       2>/dev/null \\\n105:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n106:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-child.txt\n107: rm -rf \"$T\"\n108: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-update-child.txt\n109: ```\n110: \n111: 6. Run `spec search is broken even for indexed terms` in a parent session.\n112: \n113: ```bash\n114: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n115: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"spec search is broken even for indexed terms\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-speckit-parent\"}' \\\n116:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=0 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n117:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n118:       2>/dev/null \\\n119:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n120:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-parent.txt\n121: rm -rf \"$T\"\n122: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-parent.txt\n123: ```\n124: \n125: 7. Run the same prompt in a child session.\n126: \n127: ```bash\n128: d=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; T=$(mktemp -d \"${d%/}/cl-009.XXXXXX\")\n129: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"spec search is broken even for indexed terms\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cl-009-speckit-child\"}' \\\n130:   | env AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 TMPDIR=$T SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_STATE_DIR=$T/state \\\n131:       node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js \\\n132:       2>/dev/null \\\n133:   | jq -r '.hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext' | head -2 \\\n134:   > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-child.txt\n135: rm -rf \"$T\"\n136: cat /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/cl-009-speckit-child.txt\n137: ```\n138: \n139: ### Exact Prompt Table\n140: \n141: | Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n142: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n143: | CL-009 | Brief command policy word in a parent and a child session | Confirm the policy word stays in a parent session and becomes parent-named only in a child session for a head command and for a companion command | `Run the Claude prompt hook on three prompts in a parent session and in a child session and compare the policy word each command carries.` | Commands 1 to 7 above | `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in both sessions, `/doctor:update (ask)` in the parent and `/doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child, `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child | The six two-line files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all six runs match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n144: \n145: ### Expected\n146: \n147: - `Check my git hooks setup` prints `Also: /doctor:git setup (run)` in the parent session and in the child session, because a read-only `run` command stays runnable in a child.\n148: - `update skilled to the latest version` prints `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask)` in the parent session and `system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (parent-named only)` in the child session.\n149: - `spec search is broken even for indexed terms` prints `Also: /doctor:speckit (run)` in the parent session and `Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)` in the child session.\n150: - Scores, confidences and the freshness word are read but not asserted, because they move with the index and the cache.\n151: \n152: ### Evidence\n153: \n154: Keep the six two-line files and the terminal transcript. A passing run reads like this:\n155: \n156: ```text\n157: Check my git hooks setup\n158:   parent: Also: /doctor:git setup (run)\n159:   child:  Also: /doctor:git setup (run)\n160: update skilled to the latest version\n161:   parent: Advisor: ... use system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (ask) ... pass.\n162:   child:  Advisor: ... use system-skilled-doctor → /doctor:update (parent-named only) ... pass.\n163: spec search is broken even for indexed terms\n164:   parent: Also: /doctor:speckit (run)\n165:   child:  Also: /doctor:speckit (parent-named only)\n166: ```\n167: \n168: ### Pass / Fail\n169: \n170: - **Pass**: all six policy words match Expected.\n171: - **Fail**: a policy word differs, a command or its owner changes, or a child run drops the command instead of marking it.\n172: \n173: ### Failure Triage\n174: \n175: 1. A child run keeps `ask`, `start` or a mutating `run` word: check `policyWord()` and `parentNamedOnly()` in `runtime/lib/render.ts`, then run `runtime/tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts`.\n176: 2. A companion line drops its marker: check `companionCommandLabel()` and the companion selection in `renderAdvisorBrief()`, then run `runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts`.\n177: 3. The child run matches the parent for the head command: confirm `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` sits in the environment of the `node` process, not of the shell that builds the payload.\n178: 4. The hook prints `{}` or a stale build warning: rebuild the advisor runtime with `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` and rerun the run.\n179: 5. A command or its owner changes between the two sessions: rebuild the advisor runtime and rerun, because both sessions read the same generated row.\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 4. SOURCE FILES\n184: \n185: | File | Role |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `policyWord` and `companionCommandLabel`, the two functions that choose the policy word and the child marker |\n188: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for the head command and the companion line, including the child marker |\n189: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` | Regression tests for every policy and class pair in a child session and in a parent session |\n190: \n191: ---\n192: \n193: ## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n194: \n195: - Group: CLI Hooks And Plugin\n196: - Playbook ID: CL-009\n197: - Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n198: - Feature file path: cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/cli-hooks-and-plugin/brief-command-policy-word.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760707314,"end":1794760708261}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794760709590,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"id":"prt_1e01315d6001JCY77pYXPUN9WE","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e012e68a001VFallgNABGyp5g","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1d5c0b3db4f83f4b439a003e0c9a0c89bd82e5ee","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":9811,"output":166,"reasoning":159,"cache":{"read":92544,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794760714633,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"id":"prt_1e0132989001QfmPdqXvfEcYp3","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01315db001UhZuid8Jtdt1PL","type":"step-start","snapshot":"1d5c0b3db4f83f4b439a003e0c9a0c89bd82e5ee"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760715990,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e01329d60026E1d5zo21AQPjC","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01315db001UhZuid8Jtdt1PL","type":"tool","id":"call_76aaf972a823409ba09c4c75","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md, lines 1-93\n1: ---\n2: title: \"advisor_recommend Command\"\n3: description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n6:   - \"mcp recommend tool\"\n7:   - \"native recommend\"\n8:   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n9:   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n10: version: 0.9.12.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # advisor_recommend Command\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n22: \n23: `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The disabled envelope is built by `lib/advisor-disabled.ts`, which the handler and the CLI client both call. The client calls it first, in the caller's own process, so the direct call abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off, by the same rule the adapters use: the canonical `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases such as `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED`, the master switch `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`, any truthy value or a line in the flags file. It abstains before it reaches a daemon and before the local scorer runs. A daemon that does answer reads only its own environment. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n24: \n25: Shadow comparison is response-visible but durable recording is default-off. The handler calls the shadow-delta sink only when `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` / `true`; otherwise `advisor_recommend` writes no shadow delta file.\n26: \n27: The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, which canonicalizes the path and accepts it only when it resolves under the repo root, `os.tmpdir()` or the `/tmp` symlink form, or sits in a project whose `.skilled` (or `.opencode`) links to this source tree, the way `/doctor:symlink` links one, meaning its realpath equals this source tree's own entry. The `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_WORKSPACE_ALLOWLIST` env var extends that set with extra colon-separated prefixes for CI or test runners, each canonicalized before it is trusted. The flag is unset by default, so the bounding logic stays repo-and-tmpdir-only unless an operator opts in.\n28: \n29: ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n30: \n31: Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (the `compiledRoute` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks th... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: \n33: When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n34: \n35: The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (the two `input.options?.includeCompiledRoute === false` checks in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n36: \n37: The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute`, and the first recommendation gets its `command` under every request shape. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n38: \n39: A hub below the head leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row, because `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered. The head keeps its command even then, because every caller renders the head and at most one partner.\n40: \n41: `attachCommand()` returns no command when the prompt refuses or asks about it, and no command when the prompt rules out a change. The ask or decline lead must sit right before the command, and a command named after a history lead such as `ran`, `already ran`, `was running` or `undo what` is withheld too. A command named with no such lead is offered when its owner and route gates pass. It also returns no command when the prompt asks for a correction inside an existing readme, catalog or playbook, because that request is not a call for a creation workflow.\n42: \n43: ### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n44: \n45: Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n46: \n47: `lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n48: \n49: The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n54: \n55: ### Implementation\n56: \n57: | File | Layer | Role |\n58: |---|---|---|\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n60: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.ts` | Shared | The disable switch list, its lookup and the disabled envelope the handler and the CLI client both return. |\n61: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n62: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n63: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n64: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n65: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n66: \n67: ### Validation And Tests\n68: \n69: | File | Type | Role |\n70: |---|---|---|\n71: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n72: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-disabled.vitest.ts` | Automated test | The switch names, the exact-`1` value rule and the envelope fields. |\n73: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/skill-advisor-cli-stale-daemon-retry.vitest.ts` | Automated test | The client abstains under the switch with no call to a warm daemon and no launcher, and still asks the daemon without it. |\n74: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n75: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n76: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n77: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\n78: | `Playbook scenarios [NC-001](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md), [NC-004](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md), [NC-005](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md).` | Manual playbook | Source reference |\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n83: \n84: - Group: Command surface\n85: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n86: - Feature file path: `cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md`\n87: \n88: Related references:\n89: \n90: - [02-advisor-status.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-status.md).\n91: - [03-advisor-validate.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-validate.md).\n92: - [04-compat-entrypoint.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/compat-entrypoint.md).\n93: - [feature-flag-governance.md](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) — the `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag-governance entry this enrichment's gate reads.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md, lines 1-93\n1: ---\n2: title: \"advisor_recommend Command\"\n3: description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"advisor_recommend\"\n6:   - \"mcp recommend tool\"\n7:   - \"native recommend\"\n8:   - \"skill recommendation tool\"\n9:   - \"compiledRoute enrichment\"\n10: version: 0.9.12.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # advisor_recommend Command\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n22: \n23: `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The disabled envelope is built by `lib/advisor-disabled.ts`, which the handler and the CLI client both call. The client calls it first, in the caller's own process, so the direct call abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off, by the same rule the adapters use: the canonical `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases such as `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED`, the master switch `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`, any truthy value or a line in the flags file. It abstains before it reaches a daemon and before the local scorer runs. A daemon that does answer reads only its own environment. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n24: \n25: Shadow comparison is response-visible but durable recording is default-off. The handler calls the shadow-delta sink only when `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_PATH` is set or `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_SHADOW_DELTA_ENABLED=1` / `true`; otherwise `advisor_recommend` writes no shadow delta file.\n26: \n27: The caller-supplied `workspaceRoot` is bounded by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, which canonicalizes the path and accepts it only when it resolves under the repo root, `os.tmpdir()` or the `/tmp` symlink form, or sits in a project whose `.skilled` (or `.opencode`) links to this source tree, the way `/doctor:symlink` links one, meaning its realpath equals this source tree's own entry. The `SPECKIT_ADVISOR_WORKSPACE_ALLOWLIST` env var extends that set with extra colon-separated prefixes for CI or test runners, each canonicalized before it is trusted. The flag is unset by default, so the bounding logic stays repo-and-tmpdir-only unless an operator opts in.\n28: \n29: ### Compiled-Routing Enrichment (`compiledRoute`)\n30: \n31: Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (the `compiledRoute` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks th... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: \n33: When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n34: \n35: The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (the two `input.options?.includeCompiledRoute === false` checks in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n36: \n37: The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute`, and the first recommendation gets its `command` under every request shape. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n38: \n39: A hub below the head leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row, because `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered. The head keeps its command even then, because every caller renders the head and at most one partner.\n40: \n41: `attachCommand()` returns no command when the prompt refuses or asks about it, and no command when the prompt rules out a change. The ask or decline lead must sit right before the command, and a command named after a history lead such as `ran`, `already ran`, `was running` or `undo what` is withheld too. A command named with no such lead is offered when its owner and route gates pass. It also returns no command when the prompt asks for a correction inside an existing readme, catalog or playbook, because that request is not a call for a creation workflow.\n42: \n43: ### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n44: \n45: Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n46: \n47: `lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n48: \n49: The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n54: \n55: ### Implementation\n56: \n57: | File | Layer | Role |\n58: |---|---|---|\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Handler | Source reference |\n60: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/advisor-disabled.ts` | Shared | The disable switch list, its lookup and the disabled envelope the handler and the CLI client both return. |\n61: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` | Schema | Source reference |\n62: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tools/` | Command surface | Source reference |\n63: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n64: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts` | Shared | `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` eligibility set, `DEFAULT_ON_HUBS` cohort, and the tri-state flag parser the enrichment gate reads. |\n65: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` | Script | Promoted CLI front door `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to. |\n66: \n67: ### Validation And Tests\n68: \n69: | File | Type | Role |\n70: |---|---|---|\n71: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n72: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-disabled.vitest.ts` | Automated test | The switch names, the exact-`1` value rule and the envelope fields. |\n73: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/skill-advisor-cli-stale-daemon-retry.vitest.ts` | Automated test | The client abstains under the switch with no call to a warm daemon and no launcher, and still asks the daemon without it. |\n74: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n75: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Validation reference |\n76: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/compiled-routing-consumption.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Bridge child-env allowlist forwarding, `buildNativeBrief` decision threading, and `=0` cache-invalidation coverage for the `compiledRoute` enrichment. |\n77: | `.skilled/bin/compiled-routing-flag-propagation.vitest.ts` | Automated test | Tri-state flag propagation coverage. |\n78: | `Playbook scenarios [NC-001](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md), [NC-004](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md), [NC-005](../../manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md).` | Manual playbook | Source reference |\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n83: \n84: - Group: Command surface\n85: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n86: - Feature file path: `cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md`\n87: \n88: Related references:\n89: \n90: - [02-advisor-status.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-status.md).\n91: - [03-advisor-validate.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-validate.md).\n92: - [04-compat-entrypoint.md](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/compat-entrypoint.md).\n93: - [feature-flag-governance.md](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) — the `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag-governance entry this enrichment's gate reads."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760714710,"end":1794760715990}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760715994,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0132b89001dvYlhFv5N2jJqu","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01315db001UhZuid8Jtdt1PL","type":"tool","id":"call_1fda72de1d834354a51b75f4","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md, lines 1-79\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command Meaning Matching\"\n3: description: \"A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command meaning matching\"\n6:   - \"command row embedding\"\n7:   - \"vec_cmd table\"\n8:   - \"command meaning anchor\"\n9: version: 1.0.3.0\n10: ---\n11: \n12: # Command Meaning Matching\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\n17: \n18: A command row used to pass only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. A paraphrase shares none of those words, so it reached nothing. Meaning matching adds a second way in. It works only when the local embedder runs. Without it the advisor returns the phrase-only result.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n23: \n24: ### Command Vectors\n25: \n26: `derive-command-bridges.cjs` writes an `embeddingText` for each command row the TypeScript scorer serves, 46 rows in all. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds its owned phrases. The `description` stands in only when the record states no job. A doctor row uses its router's frontmatter `description` and the route's trigger phrases. Slash command strings are stripped from the text.\n27: \n28: `skill_graph_scan` embeds that text into a separate `vec_cmd_<dim>` table. The table carries no foreign key, and the skill vector table is left untouched. A row whose text hash has not changed is skipped. A stored row that the deriver no longer generates is pruned.\n29: \n30: ### Passing on Meaning\n31: \n32: `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `fusion.ts` keeps a command row passing when it matched its own phrase. A row with no phrase match can still pass when the semantic lane reports a real embedder cosine of at least `COMMAND_MEANING_MIN_COSINE` (0.72). It must also lead the strongest other command row by `COMMAND_MEANING_MARGIN` (0.02). The row is then lifted to the confidence threshold and marked as a meaning pass. The word-overlap stand-in used under test reports its score differently, and it never counts.\n33: \n34: ### Eligibility\n35: \n36: A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A meaning-passed command row must also clear the uncertainty bar that `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` applies before the brief reports it as passing. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n37: \n38: ### Ranking\n39: \n40: `rankMeaningPassedCommandFirst` moves the best meaning-passed command to first place only when its cosine leads the top passing skill's cosine by the same 0.02. Otherwise the skill keeps its place and the command follows it.\n41: \n42: ### Measuring It\n43: \n44: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n49: \n50: ### Implementation\n51: \n52: | File | Layer | Role |\n53: |---|---|---|\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Builds each row's `embeddingText` |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json` | Generated | Carries `embeddingText` per TypeScript-served row |\n56: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/embedders/schema.ts` | Shared | Names and creates the `vec_cmd_<dim>` table |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.ts` | Shared | Embeds, skips and prunes command vectors during a scan |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Meaning pass, its two bars, eligibility and the ranking rule |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Script | The `--real-embedding` measurement |\n60: \n61: ### Validation And Tests\n62: \n63: | File | Type | Role |\n64: |---|---|---|\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 28 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Generated bridge file matches the deriver |\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n71: \n72: - Group: Scorer fusion\n73: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n74: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md`\n75: \n76: Related references:\n77: \n78: - [doctor-command-routing.md](./doctor-command-routing.md) - The phrase anchor that command rows pass on by default.\n79: - [five-lane-fusion.md](./five-lane-fusion.md) - The semantic lane that supplies the cosine.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md, lines 1-79\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command Meaning Matching\"\n3: description: \"A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command meaning matching\"\n6:   - \"command row embedding\"\n7:   - \"vec_cmd table\"\n8:   - \"command meaning anchor\"\n9: version: 1.0.3.0\n10: ---\n11: \n12: # Command Meaning Matching\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: A command row can pass on meaning when the prompt's real embedding sits close to the row's own vector, so a paraphrase that shares no words with a command can still reach it.\n17: \n18: A command row used to pass only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. A paraphrase shares none of those words, so it reached nothing. Meaning matching adds a second way in. It works only when the local embedder runs. Without it the advisor returns the phrase-only result.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n23: \n24: ### Command Vectors\n25: \n26: `derive-command-bridges.cjs` writes an `embeddingText` for each command row the TypeScript scorer serves, 46 rows in all. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds its owned phrases. The `description` stands in only when the record states no job. A doctor row uses its router's frontmatter `description` and the route's trigger phrases. Slash command strings are stripped from the text.\n27: \n28: `skill_graph_scan` embeds that text into a separate `vec_cmd_<dim>` table. The table carries no foreign key, and the skill vector table is left untouched. A row whose text hash has not changed is skipped. A stored row that the deriver no longer generates is pruned.\n29: \n30: ### Passing on Meaning\n31: \n32: `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `fusion.ts` keeps a command row passing when it matched its own phrase. A row with no phrase match can still pass when the semantic lane reports a real embedder cosine of at least `COMMAND_MEANING_MIN_COSINE` (0.72). It must also lead the strongest other command row by `COMMAND_MEANING_MARGIN` (0.02). The row is then lifted to the confidence threshold and marked as a meaning pass. The word-overlap stand-in used under test reports its score differently, and it never counts.\n33: \n34: ### Eligibility\n35: \n36: A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A meaning-passed command row must also clear the uncertainty bar that `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` applies before the brief reports it as passing. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n37: \n38: ### Ranking\n39: \n40: `rankMeaningPassedCommandFirst` moves the best meaning-passed command to first place only when its cosine leads the top passing skill's cosine by the same 0.02. Otherwise the skill keeps its place and the command follows it.\n41: \n42: ### Measuring It\n43: \n44: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n49: \n50: ### Implementation\n51: \n52: | File | Layer | Role |\n53: |---|---|---|\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Builds each row's `embeddingText` |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json` | Generated | Carries `embeddingText` per TypeScript-served row |\n56: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/embedders/schema.ts` | Shared | Names and creates the `vec_cmd_<dim>` table |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.ts` | Shared | Embeds, skips and prunes command vectors during a scan |\n58: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Meaning pass, its two bars, eligibility and the ranking rule |\n59: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Script | The `--real-embedding` measurement |\n60: \n61: ### Validation And Tests\n62: \n63: | File | Type | Role |\n64: |---|---|---|\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 28 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Generated bridge file matches the deriver |\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n71: \n72: - Group: Scorer fusion\n73: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n74: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md`\n75: \n76: Related references:\n77: \n78: - [doctor-command-routing.md](./doctor-command-routing.md) - The phrase anchor that command rows pass on by default.\n79: - [five-lane-fusion.md](./five-lane-fusion.md) - The semantic lane that supplies the cosine."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760715145,"end":1794760715994}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760715998,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0132d41001gP2BcZc8aHJIU2","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01315db001UhZuid8Jtdt1PL","type":"tool","id":"call_f8c6caa79dd24a6e83c48c12","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md, lines 1-82\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Routing\"\n3: description: \"Doctor routes from the doctor route manifest become advisor command bridges, so setup and health prompts surface a runnable /doctor:x line.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"doctor command routing\"\n6:   - \"doctor command bridges\"\n7:   - \"runnable doctor command line\"\n8:   - \"doctor setup hint\"\n9:   - \"/doctor:setup\"\n10: version: 1.0.14.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # Doctor Command Routing\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Doctor routes from the doctor route manifest become advisor command bridges, so setup and health prompts surface a runnable /doctor:x line.\n18: \n19: A passing skill keeps first place on a contested prompt and the doctor command shows as a second line. A doctor phrase listed in its route's `lead_phrases` is the exception: it ranks the doctor command first, which is how `check my environment variables` keeps `/doctor:env` ahead of system-spec-kit. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n24: \n25: ### Bridge Generation\n26: \n27: `derive-command-bridges.cjs` reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` as a third inventory source. Each route becomes one bridge `command-doctor-<command>-<target>`, or `command-doctor-<command>` when the command owns one route. Each standalone command becomes one bridge. Each `mcp_subroutes` entry becomes one bridge `command-doctor-mcp-<sub_action>`. The owning skill comes from the router's `skill_agent` tag. Each route needs a hand-written entry in both the `typescript` and `python` lists in `scoring-compatibility.json`. The deriver fills the TypeScript keywords from the manifest's trigger phrases and the Python slash markers from the runnable string, plus the bare command for a route that declares `default_target`. Every derived doctor bridge is active, `command-doctor-helper`, the three `command-doctor-agents-md-*` bridges, the three `command-doctor-symlink-*` bridges and `command-doctor-install` included, so the activation list names a bridge once and the reach probe proves every phrase it carries; a route added without its entry stays derived and unrouted until that entry lands. A route whose manifest class is `read-only` also gets `readOnly: true` in its generated bridge.\n28: \n29: ### Ranking\n30: \n31: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording, an explicit-author match on one of its manifest phrases or its runnable string. With the local embedder running it can also pass on meaning, as [command-meaning-matching.md](./command-meaning-matching.md) describes. When none passes and none was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s ranker whether the prompt describes a doctor problem, and if it does the command the ranker puts first passes by name when its policy is `run`, and `/doctor:helper` passes instead when that command asks first or has no bridge. A prompt that asks for a `repair path` or says `I have a problem`, with no ranked command and no passing skill, gets `/doctor:helper`. `stopped converging` counts as a wrong state beside a passing skill. Beside a passing skill this needs a wrong state or health check in the prompt, so ordinary requests that share doctor words get no doctor line. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. For other prompts, a passing skill stays first and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. Doctor bridges never change skill scores. The scorer computes skill ambiguity and abstention as if no doctor bridge existed. The Python native wrapper keeps the same order. A read-only question, such as a list or show prompt, holds every candidate under the read-only confidence floor, unless the prompt names the skill, a command bridge marked `readOnly` matches one of its own phrases, or a skill-specific rule lifts it. A prompt with a no-write phrase, such as `no edits` or `just list`, gets none of these lifts. Mutating bridges stay gated.\n32: \n33: ### Brief Text\n34: \n35: The renderer reads a generated lookup of runnable strings, so a doctor bridge shows as `/doctor:git setup` and never as its raw id. An unmapped doctor id renders nothing. When a doctor bridge is the top pick it is the top line. When a skill is first and a doctor bridge also passes, the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` inside the same token cap. The policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and the line is left out when the head already names that command. For no-match and outage prompts, the fallback adds one sentence naming `/doctor:setup` when the setup check matches. The check matches set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The command also checks the advisor. The Claude hook sets the flag, Pi passes it on the deadline fallback and the OpenCode plugin uses its copy of the setup check. The renderer never reads prompt text.\n36: \n37: ### Reach Probe\n38: \n39: `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` keeps the three results shown by the CLI in the reach check. Its rank-first check requires the runnable doctor command, and it rejects any activated phrase with fewer than three words. `/doctor:skill-advisor router-reach` runs it.\n40: \n41: ---\n42: \n43: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n44: \n45: ### Implementation\n46: \n47: | File | Layer | Role |\n48: |---|---|---|\n49: | `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` | Script | Doctor route manifest and trigger phrases |\n50: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Third inventory source, runtime fields and the renderer lookup block |\n51: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json` | Script | Activation of each admitted doctor bridge |\n52: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Phrase-anchor rule and skill-first ordering |\n53: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | Shared | Runnable doctor line, second line and setup hint |\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Handler | Setup-wording flag for the no-match hint |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Handler | Passes the setup flag on Pi's deadline path |\n56: | `.opencode/plugins/lib/skill-advisor-render.js` | Shared | OpenCode copy of the setup check |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Script | Python slash markers and the native wrapper order |\n58: | `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Script | Doctor reach probe |\n59: \n60: ### Validation And Tests\n61: \n62: | File | Type | Role |\n63: |---|---|---|\n64: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Doctor routing slice gate |\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-command-bridges.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Manifest-to-bridge parity |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/doctor-ordering.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Phrase anchor and skill-first ordering |\n67: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Runnable line, second line and sanitizer |\n68: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/doctor-setup-hint.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Setup hint on and off |\n69: | `Playbook scenarios [DOC-385](../../../../manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-routing-setup-onboarding.md) to [DOC-391](../../../../manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-setup-run.md).` | Manual playbook | Agent-runnable end-to-end checks |\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n74: \n75: - Group: Scorer fusion\n76: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n77: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md`\n78: \n79: Related references:\n80: \n81: - [projection.md](./projection.md) - Generated deep-loop routing projection, freshness hash guard and workflowMode publication.\n82: - [ambiguity.md](./ambiguity.md) - Top-2 ambiguity window that returns an ambiguous brief when the top two candidates are within 0.05.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md, lines 1-82\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Routing\"\n3: description: \"Doctor routes from the doctor route manifest become advisor command bridges, so setup and health prompts surface a runnable /doctor:x line.\"\n4: trig