te phrase, such as `no edits` or `just list`, gets none of these lifts. Mutating bridges stay gated.\n32: \n33: ### Brief Text\n34: \n35: The renderer reads a generated lookup of runnable strings, so a doctor bridge shows as `/doctor:git setup` and never as its raw id. An unmapped doctor id renders nothing. When a doctor bridge is the top pick it is the top line. When a skill is first and a doctor bridge also passes, the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` inside the same token cap. The policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and the line is left out when the head already names that command. For no-match and outage prompts, the fallback adds one sentence naming `/doctor:setup` when the setup check matches. The check matches set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The command also checks the advisor. The Claude hook sets the flag, Pi passes it on the deadline fallback and the OpenCode plugin uses its copy of the setup check. The renderer never reads prompt text.\n36: \n37: ### Reach Probe\n38: \n39: `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` keeps the three results shown by the CLI in the reach check. Its rank-first check requires the runnable doctor command, and it rejects any activated phrase with fewer than three words. `/doctor:skill-advisor router-reach` runs it.\n40: \n41: ---\n42: \n43: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n44: \n45: ### Implementation\n46: \n47: | File | Layer | Role |\n48: |---|---|---|\n49: | `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` | Script | Doctor route manifest and trigger phrases |\n50: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Script | Third inventory source, runtime fields and the renderer lookup block |\n51: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json` | Script | Activation of each admitted doctor bridge |\n52: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts` | Shared | Phrase-anchor rule and skill-first ordering |\n53: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | Shared | Runnable doctor line, second line and setup hint |\n54: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Handler | Setup-wording flag for the no-match hint |\n55: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Handler | Passes the setup flag on Pi's deadline path |\n56: | `.opencode/plugins/lib/skill-advisor-render.js` | Shared | OpenCode copy of the setup check |\n57: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Script | Python slash markers and the native wrapper order |\n58: | `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Script | Doctor reach probe |\n59: \n60: ### Validation And Tests\n61: \n62: | File | Type | Role |\n63: |---|---|---|\n64: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Doctor routing slice gate |\n65: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-command-bridges.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Manifest-to-bridge parity |\n66: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/doctor-ordering.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Phrase anchor and skill-first ordering |\n67: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/doctor-brief-render.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Runnable line, second line and sanitizer |\n68: | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/doctor-setup-hint.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Setup hint on and off |\n69: | `Playbook scenarios [DOC-385](../../../../manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-routing-setup-onboarding.md) to [DOC-391](../../../../manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-setup-run.md).` | Manual playbook | Agent-runnable end-to-end checks |\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n74: \n75: - Group: Scorer fusion\n76: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n77: - Feature file path: `scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md`\n78: \n79: Related references:\n80: \n81: - [projection.md](./projection.md) - Generated deep-loop routing projection, freshness hash guard and workflowMode publication.\n82: - [ambiguity.md](./ambiguity.md) - Top-2 ambiguity window that returns an ambiguous brief when the top two candidates are within 0.05."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760715585,"end":1794760715998}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794760717045,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"id":"prt_1e01332f5001T9dSColHQE8Skq","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01315db001UhZuid8Jtdt1PL","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1d5c0b3db4f83f4b439a003e0c9a0c89bd82e5ee","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":6713,"output":228,"reasoning":15,"cache":{"read":102272,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794760721667,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"id":"prt_1e0134502001OuFbD4tuWnFRdv","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01332fa0019ACoNZcN3yTvmn","type":"step-start","snapshot":"1d5c0b3db4f83f4b439a003e0c9a0c89bd82e5ee"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760726987,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e01357d2001djdpjSvnQVfOcE","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01332fa0019ACoNZcN3yTvmn","type":"tool","id":"call_20c7b24957d345719b337902","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md, lines 1-251\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system-skill-advisor v1.1.0.0, hook health without the debug flag\"\n3: description: \"v1.1.0.0 writes a prompt-free hook health record on every prompt, offers a command less often where it does not belong and renames the create-skill command row.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor v1.1.0.0\"\n6:   - \"system-skill-advisor 1.1.0.0\"\n7:   - \"hook health by default\"\n8:   - \"no-edit prompt gets no command\"\n9: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n10: contextType: \"general\"\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # v1.1.0.0, Hook Health Without the Debug Flag\n15: \n16: The prompt hook now writes a health record on every prompt. So `/doctor:setup` shows hook health with no setup. The record holds no prompt text.\n17: \n18: The brief also offers commands with more care. A prompt that rules out a change gets no command from a hub. A deep-loop command needs its own name or a request for depth.\n19: \n20: One command row also has a new id. That change breaks a caller that matches on the old id. Renamed Command below says what to change.\n21: \n22: > Spec folder: `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build`\n23: \n24: &nbsp;\n25: \n26: ## Why This Release\n27: \n28: The v1.0.0.0 hook wrote health records only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. Most machines never set it. So `advisor_status` had no hook health to report.\n29: \n30: Some briefs also offered the wrong command. A hub could offer a writing command to a prompt that asked for no edits. A mention of `/deep:review` opened the gate for `/deep:research`. A file name could read as a typed command.\n31: \n32: This release also stops offering a command where none fits the request. It adds a measure of which workflow a run opened.\n33: \n34: &nbsp;\n35: \n36: ## What's New at a Glance\n37: \n38: - **Every prompt writes a health record.** It carries no prompt, skill label or error text. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n39: - **`hookHealth` shows answers from the local scorer.** It also gives the time of the newest record. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n40: - **A hub says `(needs a mode)` only when you named it.** A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n41: - **The top hub keeps its command in the hook.** A row the brief never shows can no longer take it. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n42: - **A tool that does not show up reaches the doctor helper.** The brief names `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n43: - **A bare problem word can now lead with a doctor command.** It needs a skill that matched on shared words only. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n44: - **A no-edit prompt gets no command on any path.** More wordings now count as no-edit. <!-- section: No-Edit Prompts -->\n45: - **A refusal or a question is not a request.** A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. A command named with no such lead is, when its owner and route gates pass. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n46: - **A refusal covers a whole or-list.** `no need to run` counts, and a question mark ends the sentence before it. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n47: - **Every runtime leads with the same head.** The head row keeps its command under every request shape. <!-- section: One Head on Every Runtime -->\n48: - **The summary says which workflow a run opened.** It sorts each run into one path, such as a command document or the owning skill. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n49: - **A load now counts in more shapes.** A shell read and a prompt mirror each count, and a search does not. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n50: - **The create-skill command row has a new id.** It is now `command-create-sk-skill`. <!-- section: Renamed Command -->\n51: - **The OpenCode brief drops a repeated route line.** The head already names the hub and its command. <!-- section: OpenCode Plugin -->\n52: \n53: &nbsp;\n54: \n55: ## Hook Health\n56: \n57: #### A Record on Every Prompt\n58: \n59: The Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi and DSH hooks share one handler. That handler now appends a health record on every prompt. Before, it wrote one only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. A record holds the runtime, the status, the freshness, the duration and the cache hit. A disabled hook writes one too:\n60: \n61: ```json\n62: {\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-13T20:30:20.999Z\",\"runtime\":\"claude\",\"status\":\"skipped\",\"freshness\":\"unavailable\",\"durationMs\":1,\"cacheHit\":false}\n63: ```\n64: \n65: `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` now only adds the skill label and the error text. The log trims back to the newest 200 records once it passes 300. The OpenCode plugin writes no record.\n66: \n67: #### Health Shows Degraded Answers\n68: \n69: `advisor_status` carries `hookHealth` once any hook has written a record. It still summarizes the newest 30 records. Two fields are new. `rollingDegradedRate` is the share of answers from the CLI's local scorer. `lastRecordAt` is the time of the newest record. A runtime that stopped writing now shows its age.\n70: \n71: #### The Direct Call Reads the Switch\n72: \n73: A direct `advisor_recommend` call now abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off. It decides in the caller's own process, under `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases, the master switch or the flags file. That happens before any daemon starts or the scorer runs. The launcher passes no switch name to a daemon it starts.\n74: \n75: #### Smaller Fixes\n76: \n77: - **A runaway Python scorer child can no longer fill memory.** The brief builder keeps at most 1 MiB of each output stream.\n78: - **A busy database still gets its one retry.** The busy marker counts even past that cap or split across two writes.\n79: \n80: &nbsp;\n81: \n82: ## Brief Commands\n83: \n84: #### The Mode Label Needs Your Words\n85: \n86: A hub that cannot pick one mode says `(needs a mode)`. It now says so only when the prompt names that hub. A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. The label used to point at modes you never asked about. The head now names the hub, never a raw command row id.\n87: \n88: #### The Top Hub Keeps Its Command\n89: \n90: The hook asks for the top pick's route only. A hub used to drop its command when a lower row carried the same one. That row never reached the brief. So the command was lost. The top hub now keeps it.\n91: \n92: #### Deep-Loop Commands Need Their Own Name\n93: \n94: A deep-loop command still waits for a request for depth. Typing that same command also counts. Typing any other `/deep:` command no longer does. So a mention of `/deep:review` no longer offers `/deep:research`.\n95: \n96: #### File Names Are Not Commands\n97: \n98: A typed command must end on a word boundary. A dot or slash before more letters now continues a file name. So `/create:sk-skill.md` is no longer read as a typed command.\n99: \n100: #### A Mode Suffix Still Counts\n101: \n102: A command typed with its mode suffix reads as that command. So `/deep:review:auto` attaches `/deep:review`. The suffixes are `:auto`, `:confirm` and `:autopilot`. Any other suffix reads as no command.\n103: \n104: #### The Retired Create-Skill Name Still Reads\n105: \n106: A typed `/create:skill` now reads as `/create:sk-skill`. So a prompt that types the old name gets `sk-doc` with `/create:sk-skill (start)`. No command file answers to the old name. The retired name lives only in the `RETIRED_COMMANDS` table in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`. `runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts` covers it. A path such as `docs/create:skill.md` still reads as no command.\n107: \n108: #### The Doctor Helper for Missing Tools\n109: \n110: A prompt that says a tool does not show up now counts as a strong problem cue. The same holds when a tool does not appear. The brief then adds `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. \"Appear to\" and \"up-to-date\" stay out of the cue. A contraction typed with a curly apostrophe counts like a straight one.\n111: \n112: #### A Weak Cue Beside a Words-Only Skill\n113: \n114: A passing skill used to block the doctor hand-off unless the prompt carried a strong cue. Now a weak cue such as \"missing\" or \"drifted\" is enough when every passing skill matched on shared single words alone. The doctor ranker must still score the prompt at 10 or more. So \"the agents md file is missing the gate sections\" leads with `/doctor:agents-md check` and keeps `sk-doc` after it. A skill named by one of its own phrases still gets no doctor command on a weak cue.\n115: \n116: &nbsp;\n117: \n118: ## No-Edit Prompts\n119: \n120: #### No Command When You Rule Out a Change\n121: \n122: The scorer already held back commands on a no-edit prompt. The hub route did not. A hub could offer a writing command to \"just list\" or \"no edits\". Now one shared check covers the hub route, both scorer gates and the read-only check.\n123: \n124: #### More Wordings Count as No-Edit\n125: \n126: The check now reads \"make no changes\" and \"no changes yet\". It reads \"don't make any changes\", with or without the apostrophe. A curly apostrophe counts like a straight one. \"Don't create it\" counts too, because its object names nothing in particular. \"Don't add tools\" still asks for a change and keeps its command.\n127: \n128: One gap remains. A request that only limits its scope can also lose its command. \"Make no changes to the public API\" reads as no-edit. Losing an offer costs less than offering a wrong one.\n129: \n130: &nbsp;\n131: \n132: ## Command Fit\n133: \n134: #### A Refusal or a Question Is Not a Request\n135: \n136: A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. The lead must sit right before the command. A command named after a history lead is withheld too. Such leads are `ran`, `already ran`, `was running` and `undo what`. Any other named command is offered. Its owner and route gates still apply. A typed command is the reader's own wording. A request that states history and then asks keeps its command. `why not use` counts as that ask, and a later `run it` binds to the nearest command.\n137: \n138: #### Refusals Cover the Whole List\n139: \n140: `no need to run` and `stop using` count as refusals. A refusal over a list joined by `or` refuses every command in it. That holds for a command written as `the /x command`.\n141: \n142: #### A Question Gets No Start\n143: \n144: A question about a command gets no start offer. A question mark ends the sentence before it. A question that opens with `is the` before a command reads as a question.\n145: \n146: #### A Small Correction Keeps the Skill\n147: \n148: A small correction inside an existing readme, catalog or playbook keeps the skill and carries no creation command. The gate sits on the readme, catalog and playbook commands.\n149: \n150: #### A Weak Cue Needs a Score\n151: \n152: A weak doctor cue, a bare word that ordinary work also uses, needs a ranker score of 9. That score sits between the strong-cue bar and the no-cue bar of 10.\n153: \n154: &nbsp;\n155: \n156: ## One Head on Every Runtime\n157: \n158: #### The Head Row Keeps Its Command\n159: \n160: The head row now keeps its command under every request shape. An every-row request no longer drops it. The brief offers the same command either way. On Vibe, a head that names a command also names each command's document under `.skilled/commands/`. Vibe has no command loader.\n161: \n162: #### The OpenCode Brief Leads With the Passing Row\n163: \n164: The OpenCode plugin asks for the top-only route the hooks ask for. Its label comes from the first row that passes. The renderer leads with that row too.\n165: \n166: #### The Companion Line Names Its Policy\n167: \n168: The doctor companion line now prints its policy word. It reads `Also: /doctor:update (ask)`. It is left out when the head or its partner already prints that command.\n169: \n170: &nbsp;\n171: \n172: ## Workflow Measure\n173: \n174: #### Six Paths and Two Rates\n175: \n176: The transcript summary sorts each run into one path. `command` means it read the command's own document, or the host expanded it first. `skill` means it loaded the owning skill's `SKILL.md` or `ROUTER.md`. `otherMode` means it loaded another mode under the same hub. `asked` means the run offered the command and waited. `improvised` means it wrote without loading either. `none` means it loaded nothing and wrote nothing. `loadRate` counts the runs that loaded a workflow over every owned run. `followedRate` adds `asked` to `command` and `skill` over every owned scored run. On the stored final arm the followed rate reads claude 8 of 16 and devin 16 of 16. It reads dsh 13 of 15, opencode 14 of 16 and pi 12 of 16.\n177: \n178: #### Every Shape a Load Takes\n179: \n180: A load is a read tool, a skill tool or a shell read. The shell readers include `cat`, `sed -n` and `head`. An in-place `sed` is not a read. A `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror counts too. On Codex, one `exec_command` body counts as one shell step.\n181: \n182: #### What Does Not Count\n183: \n184: A search is not a load, and neither is a heredoc line. A write outside the checkout, such as a temp note, no longer sets the boundary for a load.\n185: \n186: #### The Counts on Sealed Rows\n187: \n188: Nobody tuned the 44 sealed plain rows. The brief carries the right command 1 time in the fixture regime. With the real embedding that count is 4. On the tuned rows the same counts are 28 of 45 and 33 of 45. The head carries a command on 8 of the 24 sealed decline rows.\n189: \n190: #### The Limits of the Reading\n191: \n192: > The rates count a workflow opened before the first write, on tuned rows with one run each, so they do not show that the brief makes a model follow a correct workflow more often.\n193: \n194: A load is not a followed step. The Codex numbers are thin counts. No brief reached Codex in either arm.\n195: \n196: #### A Paired Arm Awaits Its Runs\n197: \n198: The runner can run the same fresh rows with the advisor off and on. The scorer pairs the two arms on each row. A row only one arm scored counts as unpaired. The first run was stopped before any scoring, so it gives no rate yet.\n199: \n200: &nbsp;\n201: \n202: ## Renamed Command\n203: \n204: #### The Create-Skill Row Has a New Id\n205: \n206: **Breaking:** The command row `command-create-skill` is now `command-create-sk-skill`. Its slash markers are now `/create:sk-skill`. A typed `/create:skill` reads as `/create:sk-skill`, as Brief Commands above says. The rename itself is in the `sk-doc` v2.10.0.0 entry.\n207: \n208: &nbsp;\n209: \n210: ## OpenCode Plugin\n211: \n212: #### One Head Line in the Brief\n213: \n214: The OpenCode plugin used to push a second line with the compiled route. Its target list printed `targets=none`, because routes now carry objects. The head already names the hub, its command and its policy. So the plugin now sends the head alone.\n215: \n216: The command rename has its own entry in `sk-doc` v2.10.0.0.\n217: \n218: &nbsp;\n219: \n220: ## How It Was Checked\n221: \n222: #### Before and After\n223: \n224: | Check | Before | After |\n225: |---|---|---|\n226: | Disabled hook run, `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` unset | No record written | One `skipped` record, no prompt text |\n227: | \"make no changes, explain the readme layout\" | Not no-edit | No-edit |\n228: | \"don't edit anything, describe the hooks\", typed with a curly apostrophe | Not no-edit | No-edit |\n229: | \"don't add tools to the agent\" | Not no-edit | Not no-edit |\n230: | \"the linter does not appear in the tool list\" | No cue | Strong cue |\n231: | \"it doesn't appear to cover the routing case\" | No cue | No cue |\n232: | \"open /create:sk-skill.md and read it\" | Typed command | No typed command |\n233: | Research route on a prompt that names `/deep:review` | `/deep:research` | No command |\n234: | Research route on \"research caching in depth\" | `/deep:research` | `/deep:research` |\n235: \n236: The no-edit and cue rows ran the old and new patterns side by side. The hook row and every After cell ran on the built advisor. The other Before cells come from reading the v1.0.0.0 code. The hook run sent its log to a private temp folder.\n237: \n238: #### Tests and Scenarios\n239: \n240: - **The advisor test suite passes.** All 185 files passed. 2121 tests passed, 6 were skipped and none failed.\n241: - **The floors hold.** Typed recall reads 24 of 44 and false offers read 0 of 35.\n242: \n243: &nbsp;\n244: \n245: ## Upgrade Notes\n246: \n247: - **This release as a whole:** run `npm run build` in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` once if you run a built copy.\n248: - **If you want hook health in `/doctor:setup`:** send one prompt. Set `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` only to log the skill label and error text.\n249: - **If you read the `offered` count of the transcript summary:** a command on the partner side no longer counts as an offer. Run logs now keep `offeredAs`.\n250: - **If you match on an advisor command id:** replace `command-create-skill` with `command-create-sk-skill`.\n251: - **If you type `/create:skill`:** type `/create:sk-skill` instead. The old name has no alias.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md, lines 1-251\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system-skill-advisor v1.1.0.0, hook health without the debug flag\"\n3: description: \"v1.1.0.0 writes a prompt-free hook health record on every prompt, offers a command less often where it does not belong and renames the create-skill command row.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor v1.1.0.0\"\n6:   - \"system-skill-advisor 1.1.0.0\"\n7:   - \"hook health by default\"\n8:   - \"no-edit prompt gets no command\"\n9: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n10: contextType: \"general\"\n11: version: 1.1.0.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # v1.1.0.0, Hook Health Without the Debug Flag\n15: \n16: The prompt hook now writes a health record on every prompt. So `/doctor:setup` shows hook health with no setup. The record holds no prompt text.\n17: \n18: The brief also offers commands with more care. A prompt that rules out a change gets no command from a hub. A deep-loop command needs its own name or a request for depth.\n19: \n20: One command row also has a new id. That change breaks a caller that matches on the old id. Renamed Command below says what to change.\n21: \n22: > Spec folder: `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build`\n23: \n24: &nbsp;\n25: \n26: ## Why This Release\n27: \n28: The v1.0.0.0 hook wrote health records only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. Most machines never set it. So `advisor_status` had no hook health to report.\n29: \n30: Some briefs also offered the wrong command. A hub could offer a writing command to a prompt that asked for no edits. A mention of `/deep:review` opened the gate for `/deep:research`. A file name could read as a typed command.\n31: \n32: This release also stops offering a command where none fits the request. It adds a measure of which workflow a run opened.\n33: \n34: &nbsp;\n35: \n36: ## What's New at a Glance\n37: \n38: - **Every prompt writes a health record.** It carries no prompt, skill label or error text. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n39: - **`hookHealth` shows answers from the local scorer.** It also gives the time of the newest record. <!-- section: Hook Health -->\n40: - **A hub says `(needs a mode)` only when you named it.** A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n41: - **The top hub keeps its command in the hook.** A row the brief never shows can no longer take it. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n42: - **A tool that does not show up reaches the doctor helper.** The brief names `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n43: - **A bare problem word can now lead with a doctor command.** It needs a skill that matched on shared words only. <!-- section: Brief Commands -->\n44: - **A no-edit prompt gets no command on any path.** More wordings now count as no-edit. <!-- section: No-Edit Prompts -->\n45: - **A refusal or a question is not a request.** A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. A command named with no such lead is, when its owner and route gates pass. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n46: - **A refusal covers a whole or-list.** `no need to run` counts, and a question mark ends the sentence before it. <!-- section: Command Fit -->\n47: - **Every runtime leads with the same head.** The head row keeps its command under every request shape. <!-- section: One Head on Every Runtime -->\n48: - **The summary says which workflow a run opened.** It sorts each run into one path, such as a command document or the owning skill. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n49: - **A load now counts in more shapes.** A shell read and a prompt mirror each count, and a search does not. <!-- section: Workflow Measure -->\n50: - **The create-skill command row has a new id.** It is now `command-create-sk-skill`. <!-- section: Renamed Command -->\n51: - **The OpenCode brief drops a repeated route line.** The head already names the hub and its command. <!-- section: OpenCode Plugin -->\n52: \n53: &nbsp;\n54: \n55: ## Hook Health\n56: \n57: #### A Record on Every Prompt\n58: \n59: The Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi and DSH hooks share one handler. That handler now appends a health record on every prompt. Before, it wrote one only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set. A record holds the runtime, the status, the freshness, the duration and the cache hit. A disabled hook writes one too:\n60: \n61: ```json\n62: {\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-13T20:30:20.999Z\",\"runtime\":\"claude\",\"status\":\"skipped\",\"freshness\":\"unavailable\",\"durationMs\":1,\"cacheHit\":false}\n63: ```\n64: \n65: `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` now only adds the skill label and the error text. The log trims back to the newest 200 records once it passes 300. The OpenCode plugin writes no record.\n66: \n67: #### Health Shows Degraded Answers\n68: \n69: `advisor_status` carries `hookHealth` once any hook has written a record. It still summarizes the newest 30 records. Two fields are new. `rollingDegradedRate` is the share of answers from the CLI's local scorer. `lastRecordAt` is the time of the newest record. A runtime that stopped writing now shows its age.\n70: \n71: #### The Direct Call Reads the Switch\n72: \n73: A direct `advisor_recommend` call now abstains whenever the skill-advisor hooks are off. It decides in the caller's own process, under `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`, its aliases, the master switch or the flags file. That happens before any daemon starts or the scorer runs. The launcher passes no switch name to a daemon it starts.\n74: \n75: #### Smaller Fixes\n76: \n77: - **A runaway Python scorer child can no longer fill memory.** The brief builder keeps at most 1 MiB of each output stream.\n78: - **A busy database still gets its one retry.** The busy marker counts even past that cap or split across two writes.\n79: \n80: &nbsp;\n81: \n82: ## Brief Commands\n83: \n84: #### The Mode Label Needs Your Words\n85: \n86: A hub that cannot pick one mode says `(needs a mode)`. It now says so only when the prompt names that hub. A hub the scorer guessed shows no label. The label used to point at modes you never asked about. The head now names the hub, never a raw command row id.\n87: \n88: #### The Top Hub Keeps Its Command\n89: \n90: The hook asks for the top pick's route only. A hub used to drop its command when a lower row carried the same one. That row never reached the brief. So the command was lost. The top hub now keeps it.\n91: \n92: #### Deep-Loop Commands Need Their Own Name\n93: \n94: A deep-loop command still waits for a request for depth. Typing that same command also counts. Typing any other `/deep:` command no longer does. So a mention of `/deep:review` no longer offers `/deep:research`.\n95: \n96: #### File Names Are Not Commands\n97: \n98: A typed command must end on a word boundary. A dot or slash before more letters now continues a file name. So `/create:sk-skill.md` is no longer read as a typed command.\n99: \n100: #### A Mode Suffix Still Counts\n101: \n102: A command typed with its mode suffix reads as that command. So `/deep:review:auto` attaches `/deep:review`. The suffixes are `:auto`, `:confirm` and `:autopilot`. Any other suffix reads as no command.\n103: \n104: #### The Retired Create-Skill Name Still Reads\n105: \n106: A typed `/create:skill` now reads as `/create:sk-skill`. So a prompt that types the old name gets `sk-doc` with `/create:sk-skill (start)`. No command file answers to the old name. The retired name lives only in the `RETIRED_COMMANDS` table in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`. `runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts` covers it. A path such as `docs/create:skill.md` still reads as no command.\n107: \n108: #### The Doctor Helper for Missing Tools\n109: \n110: A prompt that says a tool does not show up now counts as a strong problem cue. The same holds when a tool does not appear. The brief then adds `/doctor:helper` beside the skills. \"Appear to\" and \"up-to-date\" stay out of the cue. A contraction typed with a curly apostrophe counts like a straight one.\n111: \n112: #### A Weak Cue Beside a Words-Only Skill\n113: \n114: A passing skill used to block the doctor hand-off unless the prompt carried a strong cue. Now a weak cue such as \"missing\" or \"drifted\" is enough when every passing skill matched on shared single words alone. The doctor ranker must still score the prompt at 10 or more. So \"the agents md file is missing the gate sections\" leads with `/doctor:agents-md check` and keeps `sk-doc` after it. A skill named by one of its own phrases still gets no doctor command on a weak cue.\n115: \n116: &nbsp;\n117: \n118: ## No-Edit Prompts\n119: \n120: #### No Command When You Rule Out a Change\n121: \n122: The scorer already held back commands on a no-edit prompt. The hub route did not. A hub could offer a writing command to \"just list\" or \"no edits\". Now one shared check covers the hub route, both scorer gates and the read-only check.\n123: \n124: #### More Wordings Count as No-Edit\n125: \n126: The check now reads \"make no changes\" and \"no changes yet\". It reads \"don't make any changes\", with or without the apostrophe. A curly apostrophe counts like a straight one. \"Don't create it\" counts too, because its object names nothing in particular. \"Don't add tools\" still asks for a change and keeps its command.\n127: \n128: One gap remains. A request that only limits its scope can also lose its command. \"Make no changes to the public API\" reads as no-edit. Losing an offer costs less than offering a wrong one.\n129: \n130: &nbsp;\n131: \n132: ## Command Fit\n133: \n134: #### A Refusal or a Question Is Not a Request\n135: \n136: A command the prompt refuses or asks about is not offered. The lead must sit right before the command. A command named after a history lead is withheld too. Such leads are `ran`, `already ran`, `was running` and `undo what`. Any other named command is offered. Its owner and route gates still apply. A typed command is the reader's own wording. A request that states history and then asks keeps its command. `why not use` counts as that ask, and a later `run it` binds to the nearest command.\n137: \n138: #### Refusals Cover the Whole List\n139: \n140: `no need to run` and `stop using` count as refusals. A refusal over a list joined by `or` refuses every command in it. That holds for a command written as `the /x command`.\n141: \n142: #### A Question Gets No Start\n143: \n144: A question about a command gets no start offer. A question mark ends the sentence before it. A question that opens with `is the` before a command reads as a question.\n145: \n146: #### A Small Correction Keeps the Skill\n147: \n148: A small correction inside an existing readme, catalog or playbook keeps the skill and carries no creation command. The gate sits on the readme, catalog and playbook commands.\n149: \n150: #### A Weak Cue Needs a Score\n151: \n152: A weak doctor cue, a bare word that ordinary work also uses, needs a ranker score of 9. That score sits between the strong-cue bar and the no-cue bar of 10.\n153: \n154: &nbsp;\n155: \n156: ## One Head on Every Runtime\n157: \n158: #### The Head Row Keeps Its Command\n159: \n160: The head row now keeps its command under every request shape. An every-row request no longer drops it. The brief offers the same command either way. On Vibe, a head that names a command also names each command's document under `.skilled/commands/`. Vibe has no command loader.\n161: \n162: #### The OpenCode Brief Leads With the Passing Row\n163: \n164: The OpenCode plugin asks for the top-only route the hooks ask for. Its label comes from the first row that passes. The renderer leads with that row too.\n165: \n166: #### The Companion Line Names Its Policy\n167: \n168: The doctor companion line now prints its policy word. It reads `Also: /doctor:update (ask)`. It is left out when the head or its partner already prints that command.\n169: \n170: &nbsp;\n171: \n172: ## Workflow Measure\n173: \n174: #### Six Paths and Two Rates\n175: \n176: The transcript summary sorts each run into one path. `command` means it read the command's own document, or the host expanded it first. `skill` means it loaded the owning skill's `SKILL.md` or `ROUTER.md`. `otherMode` means it loaded another mode under the same hub. `asked` means the run offered the command and waited. `improvised` means it wrote without loading either. `none` means it loaded nothing and wrote nothing. `loadRate` counts the runs that loaded a workflow over every owned run. `followedRate` adds `asked` to `command` and `skill` over every owned scored run. On the stored final arm the followed rate reads claude 8 of 16 and devin 16 of 16. It reads dsh 13 of 15, opencode 14 of 16 and pi 12 of 16.\n177: \n178: #### Every Shape a Load Takes\n179: \n180: A load is a read tool, a skill tool or a shell read. The shell readers include `cat`, `sed -n` and `head`. An in-place `sed` is not a read. A `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror counts too. On Codex, one `exec_command` body counts as one shell step.\n181: \n182: #### What Does Not Count\n183: \n184: A search is not a load, and neither is a heredoc line. A write outside the checkout, such as a temp note, no longer sets the boundary for a load.\n185: \n186: #### The Counts on Sealed Rows\n187: \n188: Nobody tuned the 44 sealed plain rows. The brief carries the right command 1 time in the fixture regime. With the real embedding that count is 4. On the tuned rows the same counts are 28 of 45 and 33 of 45. The head carries a command on 8 of the 24 sealed decline rows.\n189: \n190: #### The Limits of the Reading\n191: \n192: > The rates count a workflow opened before the first write, on tuned rows with one run each, so they do not show that the brief makes a model follow a correct workflow more often.\n193: \n194: A load is not a followed step. The Codex numbers are thin counts. No brief reached Codex in either arm.\n195: \n196: #### A Paired Arm Awaits Its Runs\n197: \n198: The runner can run the same fresh rows with the advisor off and on. The scorer pairs the two arms on each row. A row only one arm scored counts as unpaired. The first run was stopped before any scoring, so it gives no rate yet.\n199: \n200: &nbsp;\n201: \n202: ## Renamed Command\n203: \n204: #### The Create-Skill Row Has a New Id\n205: \n206: **Breaking:** The command row `command-create-skill` is now `command-create-sk-skill`. Its slash markers are now `/create:sk-skill`. A typed `/create:skill` reads as `/create:sk-skill`, as Brief Commands above says. The rename itself is in the `sk-doc` v2.10.0.0 entry.\n207: \n208: &nbsp;\n209: \n210: ## OpenCode Plugin\n211: \n212: #### One Head Line in the Brief\n213: \n214: The OpenCode plugin used to push a second line with the compiled route. Its target list printed `targets=none`, because routes now carry objects. The head already names the hub, its command and its policy. So the plugin now sends the head alone.\n215: \n216: The command rename has its own entry in `sk-doc` v2.10.0.0.\n217: \n218: &nbsp;\n219: \n220: ## How It Was Checked\n221: \n222: #### Before and After\n223: \n224: | Check | Before | After |\n225: |---|---|---|\n226: | Disabled hook run, `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` unset | No record written | One `skipped` record, no prompt text |\n227: | \"make no changes, explain the readme layout\" | Not no-edit | No-edit |\n228: | \"don't edit anything, describe the hooks\", typed with a curly apostrophe | Not no-edit | No-edit |\n229: | \"don't add tools to the agent\" | Not no-edit | Not no-edit |\n230: | \"the linter does not appear in the tool list\" | No cue | Strong cue |\n231: | \"it doesn't appear to cover the routing case\" | No cue | No cue |\n232: | \"open /create:sk-skill.md and read it\" | Typed command | No typed command |\n233: | Research route on a prompt that names `/deep:review` | `/deep:research` | No command |\n234: | Research route on \"research caching in depth\" | `/deep:research` | `/deep:research` |\n235: \n236: The no-edit and cue rows ran the old and new patterns side by side. The hook row and every After cell ran on the built advisor. The other Before cells come from reading the v1.0.0.0 code. The hook run sent its log to a private temp folder.\n237: \n238: #### Tests and Scenarios\n239: \n240: - **The advisor test suite passes.** All 185 files passed. 2121 tests passed, 6 were skipped and none failed.\n241: - **The floors hold.** Typed recall reads 24 of 44 and false offers read 0 of 35.\n242: \n243: &nbsp;\n244: \n245: ## Upgrade Notes\n246: \n247: - **This release as a whole:** run `npm run build` in `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` once if you run a built copy.\n248: - **If you want hook health in `/doctor:setup`:** send one prompt. Set `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` only to log the skill label and error text.\n249: - **If you read the `offered` count of the transcript summary:** a command on the partner side no longer counts as an offer. Run logs now keep `offeredAs`.\n250: - **If you match on an advisor command id:** replace `command-create-skill` with `command-create-sk-skill`.\n251: - **If you type `/create:skill`:** type `/create:sk-skill` instead. The old name has no alias."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760726482,"end":1794760726987}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760727020,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e01350e00023Z8TAC8oQv69P5","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01332fa0019ACoNZcN3yTvmn","type":"tool","id":"call_49b6f568597f456986fa91ea","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\necho \"== doctor playbook locations\"; ls -d .skilled/manual-testing-playbook .skilled/skills/manual-testing-playbook 2>&1\necho \"== doctor-commands dirs\"; ls .skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands 2>&1 | head -20; echo \"--\"; ls .skilled/skills/manual-testing-playbook 2>&1 | head\necho \"== DOC-385 file\"; ls -la .skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-routing-setup-onboarding.md 2>&1\necho \"== root manual-testing-playbook\"; ls -la manual-testing-playbook 2>&1 | head"},"output":"== doctor playbook locations\nls: .skilled/skills/manual-testing-playbook: No such file or directory\n.skilled/manual-testing-playbook\n== doctor-commands dirs\ndoctor-agents-md-block-devin-quote.md\ndoctor-agents-md-check.md\ndoctor-agents-md-cursor-mirror-load.md\ndoctor-agents-md-embed-restore.md\ndoctor-ci-report.md\ndoctor-ci-scaffold.md\ndoctor-ci-toggle-conversation.md\ndoctor-ci-toggle.md\ndoctor-deep-loop-convergence.md\ndoctor-deep-loop-empty-no-source.md\ndoctor-deep-loop-lazy-init.md\ndoctor-deep-loop-scope.md\ndoctor-env-inspect.md\ndoctor-env-save-preference.md\ndoctor-env-secret-and-per-invocation.md\ndoctor-git-hooks-list.md\ndoctor-git-hooks-switch-gate-conversation.md\ndoctor-git-hooks-switch-gate.md\ndoctor-git-setup-check.md\ndoctor-git-standards-change-rule-conversation.md\n--\nls: .skilled/skills/manual-testing-playbook: No such file or directory\n== DOC-385 file\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 michelkerkmeester  staff  6665 Nov 12 10:24 .skilled/manual-testing-playbook/doctor-commands/doctor-routing-setup-onboarding.md\n== root manual-test