{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794761000051,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0177cee002O9d0CJN7wjWZ95","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd3ffeb90jQTuAVj0Ap9","messageID":"msg_1e01753ba001D5wq5jc0JnShgi","type":"tool","id":"call_d8a3c749fe0f467c8ca7a231","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\necho \"== contract on leaf manifest\"; grep -n -A6 -B3 \"leaf-manifest\\|every leaf\\|leaf resource\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md | head -60\necho \"== helper bridge\"; grep -o \"\\\"command-doctor-helper\\\"\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json | head -2\necho \"== dsh readme in docs refs\"; grep -rn \"hooks/dsh/README\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --include=\"*.md\" --include=\"*.json\" | head"},"output":"== contract on leaf manifest\n4-trigger_phrases:\n5-  - \"which json files does a skill need\"\n6-  - \"skill root metadata contract\"\n7:  - \"leaf-manifest config aliases required\"\n8-  - \"hub versus standalone skill class\"\n9-importance_tier: normal\n10-contextType: implementation\n11-version: 1.2.10.0\n12----\n13-\n--\n46-\n47-Classification deliberately ignores every other file, including generated output. If a generated manifest could change a root's class, then a root whose manifest was never written would classify differently after regeneration, and the gate could never report that manifest as missing.\n48-\n49:`ROUTER.md` is deliberately outside classification. The root `ROUTER.md` stage-two control document is a markdown companion, not a JSON metadata file: it never appears in the required/forbidden/generated sets below, it never changes a root's class, and a hub is classified by `mode-registry.json` plus `hub-router.json` whether or not it carries a root router. `ROUTER.md` is also never a leaf, advisor identity, or typed leaf-manifest pair — it is enforced by the separate root-router contract validator, not by this metadata gate.\n50-\n51-### Current fleet\n52-\n53-| Class | Roots |\n54-| --- | --- |\n55-| **H** (7) | `cli-classifier`, `cli-external-orchestration`, `mcp-tooling`, `sk-code`, `sk-design`, `sk-doc`, `system-deep-loop` |\n--\n67-| `description.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n68-| `mode-registry.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n69-| `hub-router.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n70:| `leaf-manifest.config.json` | **forbidden** | required | authored |\n71-| `leaf-scopes.json` | optional, authored | optional, authored | authored |\n72:| `leaf-manifest.json` | required | required | **generated** |\n73-| `leaf-aliases.json` | optional, authored | required, **generated** | see §4 |\n74-| `command-metadata.json` | optional, authored | optional, authored (ownerMode = skill id) | authored |\n75-\n76-Forbidden is not stylistic. Each forbidden file would create a second source of truth for something the class already declares elsewhere: a hub carrying a standalone manifest config would hand the generator two competing inputs, and a standalone carrying registry, router, or description would claim a hub identity its packet layout cannot back.\n77-\n78-### Why `description.json` is H-only\n--\n89-\n90-### Why `leaf-scopes.json` is optional for both classes\n91-\n92:An authored scope list narrows one workflow mode's leaves to packet-relative subtrees or single docs, so a root whose configured leaf root also holds runtime code, or only partly routable docs, keeps the rest out of `leaf-manifest.json`. The generator reads the file on both paths and gives an unscoped mode its default walk — the declared leaf roots for a standalone root, `references/` and `assets/` inside the packet for a hub — so a root without it behaves exactly as it did before the file existed.\n93-\n94-It stays authored because a scope is a routing decision no generator can infer, and the presence gate neither requires nor forbids it: the file is an input to manifest generation, not a class marker. A scope that escapes the root's declared leaf roots fails closed with `OUT_OF_ROOT_LEAF_SCOPE`, and a scope naming a workflow mode the root does not declare fails closed with `ORPHAN_LEAF_SCOPE_MODE`, so a typo can neither widen a manifest nor leave a dead entry behind.\n95-\n96----\n97-\n98-## 4. GENERATED VERSUS AUTHORED\n--\n101-\n102-| File | Generated for | Why |\n103-| --- | --- | --- |\n104:| `leaf-manifest.json` | H and S | A deterministic, canonically-sorted function of the declared packets and the on-disk corpus |\n105-| `leaf-aliases.json` | **S only** | See below |\n106-\n107-`leaf-aliases.json` splits on class, and the split reflects what the two shapes actually are.\n108-\n109-A **hub's** alias rows carry real compatibility triples — they relocate a mode's resource onto a disk path outside that mode's own packet, for example `assets/changelog-template.md → sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md`. Nothing in the corpus implies those rows, so they must be authored and a generator must never overwrite them.\n110-\n111-A **standalone** root has exactly one workflow mode, so the same triple degenerates to an identity projection over its own leaves: `workflowMode` is the skill id and `leafResourceId` equals `diskPath` on every row. That is a pure function of the manifest, so deriving it is strictly better than authoring it — a hand-maintained identity list silently rots the moment a leaf is added.\n112-\n113:Everything else carries authored semantic identity, routing policy, or a declaration only a human can make: `graph-metadata.json`, `description.json`, `mode-registry.json`, `hub-router.json`, `leaf-manifest.config.json`, `command-metadata.json` and `leaf-scopes.json`, plus `leaf-aliases.json` on a hub, where the projection carries real compatibility mappings rather than a derivative. `--fix` reads every one of them and never rewrites one.\n114-\n== helper bridge\n\"command-doctor-helper\"\n\"command-doctor-helper\"\n== dsh readme in docs refs\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"== contract on leaf manifest\n4-trigger_phrases:\n5-  - \"which json files does a skill need\"\n6-  - \"skill root metadata contract\"\n7:  - \"leaf-manifest config aliases required\"\n8-  - \"hub versus standalone skill class\"\n9-importance_tier: normal\n10-contextType: implementation\n11-version: 1.2.10.0\n12----\n13-\n--\n46-\n47-Classification deliberately ignores every other file, including generated output. If a generated manifest could change a root's class, then a root whose manifest was never written would classify differently after regeneration, and the gate could never report that manifest as missing.\n48-\n49:`ROUTER.md` is deliberately outside classification. The root `ROUTER.md` stage-two control document is a markdown companion, not a JSON metadata file: it never appears in the required/forbidden/generated sets below, it never changes a root's class, and a hub is classified by `mode-registry.json` plus `hub-router.json` whether or not it carries a root router. `ROUTER.md` is also never a leaf, advisor identity, or typed leaf-manifest pair — it is enforced by the separate root-router contract validator, not by this metadata gate.\n50-\n51-### Current fleet\n52-\n53-| Class | Roots |\n54-| --- | --- |\n55-| **H** (7) | `cli-classifier`, `cli-external-orchestration`, `mcp-tooling`, `sk-code`, `sk-design`, `sk-doc`, `system-deep-loop` |\n--\n67-| `description.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n68-| `mode-registry.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n69-| `hub-router.json` | required | **forbidden** | authored |\n70:| `leaf-manifest.config.json` | **forbidden** | required | authored |\n71-| `leaf-scopes.json` | optional, authored | optional, authored | authored |\n72:| `leaf-manifest.json` | required | required | **generated** |\n73-| `leaf-aliases.json` | optional, authored | required, **generated** | see §4 |\n74-| `command-metadata.json` | optional, authored | optional, authored (ownerMode = skill id) | authored |\n75-\n76-Forbidden is not stylistic. Each forbidden file would create a second source of truth for something the class already declares elsewhere: a hub carrying a standalone manifest config would hand the generator two competing inputs, and a standalone carrying registry, router, or description would claim a hub identity its packet layout cannot back.\n77-\n78-### Why `description.json` is H-only\n--\n89-\n90-### Why `leaf-scopes.json` is optional for both classes\n91-\n92:An authored scope list narrows one workflow mode's leaves to packet-relative subtrees or single docs, so a root whose configured leaf root also holds runtime code, or only partly routable docs, keeps the rest out of `leaf-manifest.json`. The generator reads the file on both paths and gives an unscoped mode its default walk — the declared leaf roots for a standalone root, `references/` and `assets/` inside the packet for a hub — so a root without it behaves exactly as it did before the file existed.\n93-\n94-It stays authored because a scope is a routing decision no generator can infer, and the presence gate neither requires nor forbids it: the file is an input to manifest generation, not a class marker. A scope that escapes the root's declared leaf roots fails closed with `OUT_OF_ROOT_LEAF_SCOPE`, and a scope naming a workflow mode the root does not declare fails closed with `ORPHAN_LEAF_SCOPE_MODE`, so a typo can neither widen a manifest nor leave a dead entry behind.\n95-\n96----\n97-\n98-## 4. GENERATED VERSUS AUTHORED\n--\n101-\n102-| File | Generated for | Why |\n103-| --- | --- | --- |\n104:| `leaf-manifest.json` | H and S | A deterministic, canonically-sorted function of the declared packets and the on-disk corpus |\n105-| `leaf-aliases.json` | **S only** | See below |\n106-\n107-`leaf-aliases.json` splits on class, and the split reflects what the two shapes actually are.\n108-\n109-A **hub's** alias rows carry real compatibility triples — they relocate a mode's resource onto a disk path outside that mode's own packet, for example `assets/changelog-template.md → sk-create-changelog/assets/changelog-template.md`. Nothing in the corpus implies those rows, so they must be authored and a generator must never overwrite them.\n110-\n111-A **standalone** root has exactly one workflow mode, so the same triple degenerates to an identity projection over its own leaves: `workflowMode` is the skill id and `leafResourceId` equals `diskPath` on every row. That is a pure function of the manifest, so deriving it is strictly better than authoring it — a hand-maintained identity list silently rots the moment a leaf is added.\n112-\n113:Everything else carries authored semantic identity, routing policy, or a declaration only a human can make: `graph-metadata.json`, `description.json`, `mode-registry.json`, `hub-router.json`, `leaf-manifest.config.json`, `command-metadata.json` and `leaf-scopes.json`, plus `leaf-aliases.json` on a hub, where the projection carries real compatibility mappings rather than a derivative. `--fix` reads every one of them and never rewrites one.\n114-\n== helper bridge\n\"command-doctor-helper\"\n\"command-doctor-helper\"\n== dsh readme in docs refs\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\\necho \\\"== contract on leaf manifest\\\"; grep -n -A6 -B3 \\\"leaf-manifest\\\\|every leaf\\\\|leaf resource\\\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/shared/skill-root-metadata-contract.md | head -60\\necho \\\"== helper bridge\\\"; grep -o \\\"\\\\\\\"command-doctor-helper\\\\\\\"\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json | head -2\\necho \\\"== dsh readme in docs refs\\\"; grep -rn \\\"hooks/dsh/README\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --include=\\\"*.md\\\" --include=\\\"*.json\\\" | head\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760998126,"end":1794761000051}}}}
